ares paracetamol with NSAIDs?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pain/fever relief without the typical NSAID platelet/GI effects","explanation":"Paracetamol lacks the typical NSAID platelet/GI effects but still has important exposure-related liver risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Equivalent GI bleeding risk but no dose-related liver risk","explanation":"This reverses the distinction and omits the major liver hazard."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain/fever relief through the same platelet inhibition as aspirin","explanation":"Paracetamol does not share the typical NSAID platelet effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Inflammation control with stronger peripheral COX inhibition","explanation":"Paracetamol acts predominantly as a central analgesic/antipyretic, rather than a stronger peripheral anti-inflammatory drug."}],"point":"Distinguish paracetamol from the typical platelet/GI effects of NSAIDs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-acamol--standard--acetam_nsaid_distinction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acetam_nsaid_distinction"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which history item is specifically relevant to the liver-risk review before paracetamol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Aspirin-associated bronchospasm","explanation":"This is important when considering NSAIDs; it is not the liver-risk factor asked about."},{"id":"B","text":"Heavy alcohol use","explanation":"This is specifically relevant to the liver-risk review described for paracetamol."},{"id":"C","text":"Previous symptomatic bradycardia","explanation":"This matters for rate-slowing drugs, but is not the paracetamol liver-risk factor asked about."},{"id":"D","text":"Previous peptic-ulcer bleeding","explanation":"This is an important medication history, particularly for NSAIDs, but is not the liver-risk factor asked about."}],"point":"Include heavy alcohol use in paracetamol safety assessment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-acamol--standard--acetam_alcohol_review","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acetam_alcohol_review"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":9},{"id":"ibd2-acamol--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-acamol","itemType":"medication","title":"ACAMOL — paracetamol (acetaminophen)","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A prescribed acetaminophen dose is due. The patient has also used an acetaminophen-containing combination analgesic. Which exposure should be compared with the patient-specific limit?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Acetaminophen since the last meal in that 24 hours","explanation":"Meals do not reset cumulative drug exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"Acetaminophen from the current prescription in that 24 hours","explanation":"This omits additional exposure from the combination medicine."},{"id":"C","text":"Acetaminophen from the highest-strength product in that 24 hours","explanation":"The lower-strength preparation also contributes."},{"id":"D","text":"Total acetaminophen from all products in the relevant 24 hours","explanation":"All sources contribute to cumulative exposure."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Count acetaminophen from every product before assessing total exposure.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-acamol--advanced--acetaminophen_total","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acetaminophen_total"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient reports a potentially excessive amount of ACAMOL — paracetamol (acetaminophen) but currently has few symptoms. Which feature explains the need for prompt assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Current temperature predicts the eventual hepatic response","explanation":"Normal temperature does not exclude overdose injury."},{"id":"B","text":"Analgesic benefit predicts the eventual hepatic response","explanation":"Pain relief cannot establish that the exposure was safe."},{"id":"C","text":"Significant hepatic injury can have delayed manifestations","explanation":"Early well-being does not exclude serious toxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"An absence of vomiting indicates rapid drug clearance","explanation":"Lack of vomiting does not establish adequate clearance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Suspected acetaminophen overdose needs assessment even before symptoms develop.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-acamol--advanced--hepatic_overdose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::hepatic_overdose"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A frail patient with liver disease has a total acetaminophen exposure below a commonly quoted adult maximum. What still needs review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The patient-specific safe limit rather than the generic maximum alone","explanation":"Frailty and liver disease may lower the appropriate limit."},{"id":"B","text":"The assumption that absence of pain eliminates hepatic risk","explanation":"Analgesic need does not determine hepatic safety."},{"id":"C","text":"The assumption that combination-product acetaminophen need not count","explanation":"All sources contribute to total exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"The next dose based on the generic maximum without patient factors","explanation":"A universal maximum is not safe for every patient."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review the patient-specific acetaminophen limit in frailty and liver disease.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-acamol--advanced--acen_patient_limit","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acen_patient_limit"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":3},{"id":"ibd2-acamol--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-acamol","itemType":"medication","title":"ACAMOL — paracetamol (acetaminophen)","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient brings two pain medicines and a cold remedy while prescribed ACAMOL — paracetamol (acetaminophen). What prevents an unintended overdose?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Count only the medicine prescribed for pain","explanation":"A cold remedy may also contain acetaminophen."},{"id":"B","text":"Calculate each product's daily limit separately","explanation":"Separate product limits can hide combined overdose."},{"id":"C","text":"Count only products taken at the same time","explanation":"Doses across the full relevant period contribute to exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Add acetaminophen amounts from every product","explanation":"Different products may contribute the same ingredient."}],"point":"Count acetaminophen from every product before assessing total exposure.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-acamol--7-day--acetaminophen_total","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acetaminophen_total"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient reports a potentially toxic acetaminophen ingestion but feels well. What should happen next?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Review only if abdominal pain develops","explanation":"Symptoms can be absent early after overdose."},{"id":"B","text":"Reassure because the patient is alert","explanation":"Alertness does not exclude acetaminophen toxicity."},{"id":"C","text":"Urgent toxicity assessment despite absent symptoms","explanation":"Early well-being does not exclude evolving liver injury."},{"id":"D","text":"Wait for jaundice before requesting review","explanation":"Waiting for established injury delays assessment."}],"point":"Suspected acetaminophen overdose needs assessment even before symptoms develop.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-acamol--7-day--hepatic_overdose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::hepatic_overdose"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which note best documents benefit after prescribed ACAMOL — paracetamol (acetaminophen) for pain?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The medication chart contains a signed entry","explanation":"A signature confirms administration, not effectiveness."},{"id":"B","text":"Pain fell from 7 to 3 and movement became easier","explanation":"This records symptom and functional response."},{"id":"C","text":"The scheduled dose was administered at 10:00","explanation":"Administration is not evidence of benefit."},{"id":"D","text":"The patient swallowed the medicine without difficulty","explanation":"Swallowing does not establish analgesic response."}],"point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-acamol--7-day--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::pain_response"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-acamol","title":"ACETAMINOPHEN TABLET [MAJOR PHARMACEUTICALS]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=76cb8209-6c43-322e-e053-2991aa0a51eb"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Non-opioid analgesic and antipyretic.","mechanism":"Predominantly central analgesic/antipyretic action involving inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis in the CNS.","use":"Mild-to-moderate pain and fever; in orthopaedics it is commonly part of multimodal pain control after injury or surgery.","administration":"Give only by the ordered route/formulation. Before administering, calculate the patient's total paracetamol/acetaminophen exposure from ALL products in the previous 24 hours. Do not assume a fixed universal maximum for every patient; liver disease, alcohol use, frailty and other factors can lower the safe limit.","monitor":"• Assess pain/temperature before and after administration. • Check for duplicate paracetamol/acetaminophen in combination products. • Review liver disease, heavy alcohol use and total 24-hour exposure. • Escalate suspected overdose promptly because serious hepatotoxicity can be delayed.","why":"Paracetamol provides analgesia and antipyresis without the platelet/GI effects typical of NSAIDs, but excessive total exposure can cause severe liver injury.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Acamol tablets contain paracetamol; a common marketed tablet is 500 mg, but verify the exact ward pack and strength before administration."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Non-opioid analgesic and antipyretic.","id":"section-0-97aa2c209f83b77d"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Predominantly central analgesic/antipyretic action involving inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis in the CNS.","id":"section-1-d04ecbab35b3e972"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Mild-to-moderate pain and fever; in orthopaedics it is commonly part of multimodal pain control after injury or surgery.","id":"section-2-f9a05cb6cb9998f3"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Give only by the ordered route/formulation. Before administering, calculate the patient's total paracetamol/acetaminophen exposure from ALL products in the previous 24 hours. Do not assume a fixed universal maximum for every patient; liver disease, alcohol use, frailty and other factors can lower the safe limit.","id":"section-3-0e21f0aac72c9411"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Assess pain/temperature before and after administration. • Check for duplicate paracetamol/acetaminophen in combination products. • Review liver disease, heavy alcohol use and total 24-hour exposure. • Escalate suspected overdose promptly because serious hepatotoxicity can be delayed.","id":"section-4-8c0b9c7e37b76a71"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Paracetamol provides analgesia and antipyresis without the platelet/GI effects typical of NSAIDs, but excessive total exposure can cause severe liver injury.","id":"section-5-83ca2568b731928c"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Acamol tablets contain paracetamol; a common marketed tablet is 500 mg, but verify the exact ward pack and strength before administration.","id":"section-6-ac05d188d458a145"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-trufen","title":"TRUFEN — ibuprofen","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard","itemId":"ibd2-trufen","itemType":"medication","title":"TRUFEN — ibuprofen","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of TRUFEN — ibuprofen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits cyclooxygenase and reduces prostaglandins","explanation":"Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Enhances GABA-A receptor activity","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Predominantly alpha1-mediated vasoconstriction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Relaxes arterial smooth muscle","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does TRUFEN — ibuprofen belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Non-opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: NSAID; analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory."},{"id":"B","text":"Opioid/monoamine analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: NSAID; analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory."},{"id":"C","text":"Opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: NSAID; analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory."},{"id":"D","text":"NSAID","explanation":"NSAID; analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of TRUFEN — ibuprofen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Replacement therapy for an adrenal crisis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Relief of musculoskeletal pain and inflammation","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Mild-to-moderate musculoskeletal pain and inflammation, including painful swelling after soft-tissue injury and orthopaedic conditions."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which new finding in a patient taking TRUFEN — ibuprofen most strongly suggests an upper-GI complication?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dry, hard stool","explanation":"This suggests constipation rather than characteristic upper-GI blood loss."},{"id":"B","text":"Small, formed stool","explanation":"This does not identify the bleeding pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Black, tarry stool","explanation":"Melena can indicate upper gastrointestinal bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"Pale, greasy stool","explanation":"This points toward a different gastrointestinal process."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_gi"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which situation makes the renal safety of TRUFEN — ibuprofen particularly important to reassess?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Improved appetite with stable weight","explanation":"This does not identify a new renal-perfusion concern."},{"id":"B","text":"Vomiting with volume depletion","explanation":"Reduced effective circulating volume increases vulnerability to NSAID renal effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Improved mobility with controlled pain","explanation":"This reflects recovery rather than increased renal risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Stable intake with unchanged creatinine","explanation":"This does not suggest the same new renal vulnerability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_renal"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which previous reaction most directly changes the hypersensitivity assessment before TRUFEN — ibuprofen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bronchospasm after aspirin","explanation":"This may indicate a significant reaction relevant to another NSAID."},{"id":"B","text":"Dry cough during an ACE inhibitor","explanation":"This is a different treatment-related effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Nausea after an opioid","explanation":"This is a different drug effect and does not identify NSAID hypersensitivity."},{"id":"D","text":"Flushing during vancomycin infusion","explanation":"This is a different drug-associated reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_allergy"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which new finding is consistent with an important volume-related adverse effect of TRUFEN — ibuprofen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced JVP with stable breathing","explanation":"This does not identify worsening retention."},{"id":"B","text":"Improved orthopnea with less edema","explanation":"This is a congestion-improvement pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Ankle edema with a BP rise","explanation":"NSAIDs can worsen fluid retention and blood pressure."},{"id":"D","text":"Weight loss with increased urine output","explanation":"This suggests a different volume pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"NSAIDs can worsen fluid retention and cardiovascular risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--nsaid_cv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_cv"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"What distinguishes Trufen Extra from plain Trufen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"It replaces ibuprofen with tramadol","explanation":"This is not the combination associated with this interaction."},{"id":"B","text":"It also contains paracetamol","explanation":"The combination requires counting both ibuprofen and paracetamol exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"It replaces ibuprofen with prednisolone","explanation":"The product is not described as a corticosteroid replacement."},{"id":"D","text":"It also contains warfarin","explanation":"This is not the stated combination."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Distinguish plain Trufen from Trufen Extra by its added paracetamol.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--trufen_extra","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::trufen_extra"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which paired reassessment directly measures the intended analgesic response to TRUFEN — ibuprofen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Stool frequency and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These assess bowel effects rather than analgesic benefit."},{"id":"B","text":"BP and pulse during a postural change","explanation":"These assess hemodynamic tolerance rather than the pain response."},{"id":"C","text":"Alertness and respiratory effort during rest","explanation":"These assess important adverse effects rather than analgesic benefit itself."},{"id":"D","text":"Pain intensity and ability to perform the agreed activity","explanation":"These compare pain and function with baseline."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::pain_response"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"In addition to GI bleeding, renal injury, and fluid retention, which risk is associated with ibuprofen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Progressive pulmonary fibrosis","explanation":"This is not the additional risk identified."},{"id":"B","text":"Immune-mediated thrombocytopenia","explanation":"This is not the additional risk identified."},{"id":"C","text":"Serious cardiovascular events","explanation":"Increased cardiovascular risk is a separate NSAID precaution in this case."},{"id":"D","text":"Hemorrhagic bladder injury","explanation":"This is not the additional risk identified."}],"point":"Recognize cardiovascular risk as a distinct NSAID precaution.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--standard--ibuprofen_cv_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::ibuprofen_cv_risk"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":10},{"id":"ibd2-trufen--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-trufen","itemType":"medication","title":"TRUFEN — ibuprofen","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient develops melena during TRUFEN — ibuprofen treatment. Which drug action best explains the gastrointestinal vulnerability?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced vitamin-K-dependent clotting-factor synthesis","explanation":"This is the principal anticoagulant mechanism of warfarin."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced protective gastric prostaglandin activity","explanation":"COX inhibition can impair mucosal protection."},{"id":"C","text":"Increased gastric proton-pump inhibition","explanation":"Acid suppression does not explain NSAID mucosal injury."},{"id":"D","text":"Blocked platelet P2Y12 receptor activation","explanation":"This describes a different antiplatelet mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-trufen--advanced--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_gi"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A dehydrated patient taking TRUFEN — ibuprofen has rising creatinine. Which mechanism best explains the concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Direct mineralocorticoid-receptor blockade","explanation":"This is the principal action of spironolactone."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased renal glucose and sodium excretion","explanation":"This is the mechanism of SGLT2 inhibitors rather than NSAIDs."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced prostaglandin support of renal perfusion","explanation":"NSAIDs can impair compensatory renal blood flow."},{"id":"D","text":"Enhanced antithrombin inhibition of factor Xa","explanation":"This is an anticoagulant mechanism, not the renal NSAID effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--advanced--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_renal"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient developed wheeze after two different NSAIDs and is now prescribed TRUFEN — ibuprofen. Which feature of the history matters most?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A reaction restricted to a local skin application","explanation":"That does not describe the systemic respiratory pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"An isolated gastrointestinal intolerance to one preparation","explanation":"That is not the history described."},{"id":"C","text":"A reaction restricted to an IV infusion rate","explanation":"The history describes reactions to related drugs, not just rate-related intolerance."},{"id":"D","text":"A recurrent respiratory reaction within a related drug group","explanation":"It warrants review of potential NSAID-associated hypersensitivity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-trufen--advanced--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_allergy"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"An NSAID-treated patient reports better joint pain but more edema and breathlessness. Which paired interpretation best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Improved decongestion with persistent pain","explanation":"This reverses both changes reported in the case."},{"id":"B","text":"Analgesic failure with improving cardiac perfusion","explanation":"Pain improved, and the new symptoms do not establish better perfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced extracellular volume with ongoing inflammation","explanation":"Edema and breathlessness suggest a different volume concern."},{"id":"D","text":"Analgesic benefit with possible worsening congestion","explanation":"The desired effect and fluid-related harm can coexist."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"NSAIDs can worsen fluid retention and cardiovascular risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--advanced--nsaid_cv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_cv"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient is prescribed TRUFEN — ibuprofen and reports taking an additional NSAID bought outside the hospital. Which issue needs clarification?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Overlapping NSAID exposure and adverse-effect risk","explanation":"Two NSAIDs can duplicate GI and renal risk without being an intended combination."},{"id":"B","text":"A need to add an antiplatelet indication","explanation":"Taking an NSAID does not establish such an indication."},{"id":"C","text":"A reduction in total COX-inhibitor exposure","explanation":"Adding another NSAID does not reduce the shared exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"A need to interpret the combination as opioid tolerance","explanation":"Neither the report nor the drug group establishes opioid tolerance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Avoid unintended duplication of NSAIDs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--advanced--duplicate_nsaid","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::duplicate_nsaid"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A postoperative patient’s pain and swelling have improved on ibuprofen. Which plan best limits unnecessary drug exposure?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increase the interval only after the full prescribed course ends","explanation":"Dose and duration should be reviewed as treatment needs change."},{"id":"B","text":"Review the lowest effective ordered dose and shortest needed course","explanation":"Both dose and duration should be kept appropriate to the individual treatment goal."},{"id":"C","text":"Keep the current dose until all swelling has disappeared","explanation":"A single symptom does not justify continuing unchanged exposure without review."},{"id":"D","text":"Keep the current dose until the entire packet is finished","explanation":"Packet size does not determine the required treatment duration."}],"point":"Use the lowest effective ordered NSAID dose for the shortest appropriate duration.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--advanced--ibuprofen_minimum_exposure","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::ibuprofen_minimum_exposure"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-trufen--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-trufen","itemType":"medication","title":"TRUFEN — ibuprofen","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During TRUFEN — ibuprofen treatment a patient passes black tarry stool. Which action is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Check the pain score before deciding to report","explanation":"Pain relief does not make possible bleeding safe."},{"id":"B","text":"Document the stool as an expected analgesic effect","explanation":"Melena is an adverse warning, not the intended effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Offer food with the next dose and observe","explanation":"Food does not resolve suspected bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"Assess bleeding and circulation and escalate promptly","explanation":"Melena can indicate gastrointestinal bleeding."}],"point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-trufen--7-day--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_gi"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An NSAID-treated patient has vomiting and poor intake. Which complication becomes more likely?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Protection against kidney injury from less urine","explanation":"Oliguria can indicate deterioration, not protection."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased kidney clearance from dehydration","explanation":"Dehydration does not improve renal clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"Acute kidney injury from reduced renal perfusion","explanation":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-related renal risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Improved filtration from prostaglandin inhibition","explanation":"Prostaglandin inhibition can compromise renal perfusion."}],"point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--7-day--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_renal"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Before TRUFEN — ibuprofen, a patient recalls wheezing and facial swelling after aspirin. What should the nurse do?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Separate it from other medicines by two hours","explanation":"Timing separation does not prevent NSAID hypersensitivity."},{"id":"B","text":"Clarify the reaction before administering the NSAID","explanation":"Wheeze and facial swelling may signal serious hypersensitivity."},{"id":"C","text":"Give it with food and monitor dyspepsia","explanation":"Food does not prevent an allergic or bronchospastic reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Use a smaller dose without clarifying the reaction","explanation":"A reduced dose does not establish hypersensitivity safety."}],"point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-trufen--7-day--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_allergy"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking TRUFEN — ibuprofen also uses an over-the-counter anti-inflammatory. What should be checked?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use both if the first does not fully relieve pain","explanation":"Unplanned duplication needs prescription review rather than automatic addition."},{"id":"B","text":"Identify overlapping NSAID ingredients and exposure","explanation":"Duplication can increase gastrointestinal and renal harm."},{"id":"C","text":"Count only prescribed anti-inflammatory doses","explanation":"Nonprescription doses also contribute exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Separate the two NSAIDs by a meal","explanation":"Meal separation does not remove duplicate pharmacological exposure."}],"point":"Avoid unintended duplication of NSAIDs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--7-day--duplicate_nsaid","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::duplicate_nsaid"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which note best documents benefit after prescribed TRUFEN — ibuprofen for pain?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pain fell from 7 to 3 and movement became easier","explanation":"This records symptom and functional response."},{"id":"B","text":"The patient swallowed the medicine without difficulty","explanation":"Swallowing does not establish analgesic response."},{"id":"C","text":"The medication chart contains a signed entry","explanation":"A signature confirms administration, not effectiveness."},{"id":"D","text":"The scheduled dose was administered at 10:00","explanation":"Administration is not evidence of benefit."}],"point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-trufen--7-day--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::pain_response"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":5}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-trufen","title":"IBUPROFEN TABLET, FILM COATED [PD-RX PHARMACEUTICALS, INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=606f839a-d4b3-4437-8ea1-58f41c484d6f"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"NSAID; analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory.","mechanism":"Reversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, reducing prostaglandin synthesis.","use":"Mild-to-moderate musculoskeletal pain and inflammation, including painful swelling after soft-tissue injury and orthopaedic conditions.","administration":"PO when the ordered product is a tablet. Verify the exact strength and whether it is plain Trufen or Trufen Extra. Use the lowest effective ordered dose for the shortest appropriate duration; do not duplicate with another NSAID unless specifically intended.","monitor":"• Assess pain and inflammation before/after. • Watch for GI pain, melena/hematemesis or other bleeding. • Review renal function, hydration and concurrent nephrotoxic drugs. • Check for NSAID/aspirin hypersensitivity or bronchospasm history. • NSAIDs can worsen fluid retention/BP and increase cardiovascular risk. • Verify combination products to avoid accidental extra paracetamol.","why":"COX inhibition reduces prostaglandins, improving pain and inflammation but also reducing gastric protection and renal prostaglandin effects.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Palestinian MoH records Trufen 200 mg and 600 mg tablets as ibuprofen. IMPORTANT: Trufen Extra is a different combination product containing ibuprofen + paracetamol, so read the package before giving."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"NSAID; analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory.","id":"section-0-2159478ee8bb4344"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Reversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, reducing prostaglandin synthesis.","id":"section-1-f8965b02108ebcdd"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Mild-to-moderate musculoskeletal pain and inflammation, including painful swelling after soft-tissue injury and orthopaedic conditions.","id":"section-2-3111790c29d0616d"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"PO when the ordered product is a tablet. Verify the exact strength and whether it is plain Trufen or Trufen Extra. Use the lowest effective ordered dose for the shortest appropriate duration; do not duplicate with another NSAID unless specifically intended.","id":"section-3-e1faf95123c64e20"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Assess pain and inflammation before/after. • Watch for GI pain, melena/hematemesis or other bleeding. • Review renal function, hydration and concurrent nephrotoxic drugs. • Check for NSAID/aspirin hypersensitivity or bronchospasm history. • NSAIDs can worsen fluid retention/BP and increase cardiovascular risk. • Verify combination products to avoid accidental extra paracetamol.","id":"section-4-f4660c1363af6f79"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"COX inhibition reduces prostaglandins, improving pain and inflammation but also reducing gastric protection and renal prostaglandin effects.","id":"section-5-04ce5ce3d6ca38cc"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Palestinian MoH records Trufen 200 mg and 600 mg tablets as ibuprofen. IMPORTANT: Trufen Extra is a different combination product containing ibuprofen + paracetamol, so read the package before giving.","id":"section-6-b69d497d36595ecb"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-cefazolin","title":"CEFAZOLIN","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard","itemId":"ibd2-cefazolin","itemType":"medication","title":"CEFAZOLIN","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of CEFAZOLIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"B","text":"Predominantly alpha1-mediated vasoconstriction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"C","text":"Inhibits serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces GI/GU smooth-muscle spasm","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does CEFAZOLIN belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Lincosamide","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."},{"id":"B","text":"Fluoroquinolone","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."},{"id":"C","text":"Polyene antifungal","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."},{"id":"D","text":"First-generation cephalosporin","explanation":"First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of CEFAZOLIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Relief of bronchospasm by beta2 stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Perioperative prophylaxis or susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Highly relevant to orthopaedics for perioperative antibacterial prophylaxis and for treatment of susceptible bacterial infections when prescribed."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Minutes after starting CEFAZOLIN, a patient develops wheeze, facial swelling, and hypotension. Which reaction best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated infusion-site phlebitis","explanation":"A local vein reaction does not explain the multisystem findings."},{"id":"B","text":"Delayed marrow suppression","explanation":"This does not fit the rapid onset or the described manifestations."},{"id":"C","text":"Anaphylaxis","explanation":"Acute airway and circulatory compromise after exposure is strongly concerning."},{"id":"D","text":"Antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"This does not explain the immediate airway and circulatory pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which result best informs assessment of accumulation risk and possible regimen adjustment for CEFAZOLIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Creatinine trend and estimated GFR","explanation":"These inform renal clearance and regimen review."},{"id":"B","text":"ALT trend and serum bilirubin","explanation":"These assess hepatic injury rather than directly estimate renal clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"TSH trend and free thyroxine","explanation":"These assess thyroid function rather than renal elimination."},{"id":"D","text":"INR trend and prothrombin time","explanation":"These assess a coagulation pathway rather than renal clearance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"During CEFAZOLIN therapy, which new bowel pattern most warrants assessment for antibiotic-associated colitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Normal formed stool after breakfast","explanation":"This is not a new concerning diarrhea pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Frequent, persistent watery diarrhea","explanation":"Persistent diarrhea during antibiotic exposure needs assessment for C. difficile and other causes."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced stool frequency during immobility","explanation":"This does not identify the complication being tested."},{"id":"D","text":"Hard stool with straining","explanation":"This points toward constipation rather than colitis-related diarrhea."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"When CEFAZOLIN is ordered for perioperative prophylaxis, which event determines the key timing relationship?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The surgical incision","explanation":"Protective antibacterial exposure is needed during operative contamination risk."},{"id":"B","text":"The first postoperative fever","explanation":"Waiting for fever would not meet a prophylactic purpose."},{"id":"C","text":"The first postoperative dressing change","explanation":"The protective aim precedes this later event."},{"id":"D","text":"The first postoperative meal","explanation":"Resumption of food is not the principal prophylaxis timing target."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Perioperative antibiotic timing should provide tissue exposure at incision.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--prophylaxis_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::prophylaxis_timing"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A bacterial infection is being treated with CEFAZOLIN. Which paired findings most directly inform whether the antimicrobial plan remains suitable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Appetite and bowel frequency","explanation":"These inform general tolerance but not the organism-directed treatment decision."},{"id":"B","text":"Infection response and culture susceptibility","explanation":"Clinical progress and organism susceptibility inform appropriateness."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain response and analgesic requirement","explanation":"These can improve without establishing antimicrobial susceptibility."},{"id":"D","text":"Fluid balance and serum sodium","explanation":"These inform hydration but do not establish antimicrobial appropriateness."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"An IV site becomes painful and erythematous during CEFAZOLIN. What local complication should be assessed?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An expected sign of infection resolution","explanation":"Clinical improvement is not established by a new IV-site reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"A systemic electrolyte correction","explanation":"A local inflamed site does not establish electrolyte response."},{"id":"C","text":"Infusion-site irritation or phlebitis","explanation":"Pain and erythema along a cannulated vein need local assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"A therapeutic increase in tissue exposure","explanation":"Local pain and redness are not proof of a beneficial drug effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Inspect an IV site for treatment-related irritation or phlebitis.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--iv_site","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::iv_site"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which bacterial target does cefazolin bind?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bacterial dihydrofolate reductase","explanation":"This is a folate-pathway target, not cefazolin’s target."},{"id":"B","text":"Penicillin-binding proteins","explanation":"Binding these proteins inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis."},{"id":"C","text":"Bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit","explanation":"This is a protein-synthesis target, not cefazolin’s target."},{"id":"D","text":"Bacterial DNA gyrase","explanation":"This is associated with fluoroquinolone action, not cefazolin’s target."}],"point":"Identify penicillin-binding proteins as cefazolin’s taught bacterial target.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--cefazolin_pbp","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_pbp"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":10},{"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-cefazolin","itemType":"medication","title":"CEFAZOLIN","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"Before CEFAZOLIN, which prior antibiotic reaction carries the strongest immediate hypersensitivity concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Local IV discomfort without systemic symptoms","explanation":"This is less specific for a serious immediate systemic allergy."},{"id":"B","text":"Metallic taste during metronidazole therapy","explanation":"This is a different, commonly nonallergic adverse effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Rapid wheeze and collapse after a beta-lactam","explanation":"The timing and cardiorespiratory involvement suggest a serious immediate reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Diarrhea several days into clindamycin therapy","explanation":"This needs review but is not the same immediate reaction pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Renal clearance falls during CEFAZOLIN therapy while the dose and interval remain unchanged. Which pharmacokinetic change is most concerning?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A shorter elimination half-life","explanation":"Reduced clearance tends to prolong, not shorten, elimination."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased drug accumulation","explanation":"Reduced renal elimination can raise exposure between doses."},{"id":"C","text":"Decreased drug accumulation","explanation":"This is the opposite of the expected effect of reduced clearance."},{"id":"D","text":"A lower steady-state exposure","explanation":"With unchanged dosing, reduced clearance can increase exposure."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient develops persistent watery diarrhea after CEFAZOLIN. Which explanation should be considered during assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An immediate IgE-mediated airway reaction","explanation":"The case describes a gastrointestinal pattern rather than acute anaphylaxis."},{"id":"B","text":"An intended increase in osmotic laxative activity","explanation":"The antibiotic is not being used as an osmotic laxative."},{"id":"C","text":"Therapeutic stimulation of intestinal glucose absorption","explanation":"This does not explain the antibiotic-associated diarrhea warning."},{"id":"D","text":"Antibiotic-associated disturbance of intestinal flora","explanation":"This can permit C. difficile-associated disease."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Surgery is delayed after CEFAZOLIN prophylaxis was scheduled. Which relationship needs review under the surgical protocol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose timing relative to the next pain score","explanation":"Analgesic assessment does not determine antibacterial exposure at incision."},{"id":"B","text":"Dose timing relative to the next bowel movement","explanation":"Bowel timing does not establish prophylactic surgical exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"Dose timing relative to the first mobilization attempt","explanation":"Mobilization is not the defining perioperative prophylaxis timing target."},{"id":"D","text":"Dose timing relative to the revised incision time","explanation":"The delay may change whether exposure matches the operative risk period."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Perioperative antibiotic timing should provide tissue exposure at incision.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--prophylaxis_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::prophylaxis_timing"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Two cefazolin products are supplied with different preparation instructions. What determines which dilution and IV method to use?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The dilution used for the last cefazolin patient","explanation":"A previous patient’s product and order may differ."},{"id":"B","text":"The supplied product instructions and the authorized order","explanation":"Preparation and IV administration are product- and order-specific."},{"id":"C","text":"The method used for any cefazolin vial of that strength","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish identical preparation or administration instructions."},{"id":"D","text":"The method used for another cephalosporin at the same dose","explanation":"Related antibiotics are not automatically interchangeable in preparation."}],"point":"Verify the exact cefazolin product and order before selecting preparation and IV administration instructions.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--cefazolin_product_preparation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_product_preparation"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Why does cefazolin prophylaxis need to be related to incision time?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"To provide adequate tissue antibiotic levels when contamination may occur","explanation":"The rationale is adequate tissue exposure at incision."},{"id":"B","text":"To prevent the kidney from eliminating any antibiotic during surgery","explanation":"Elimination continues; it is not the reason for incision-related timing."},{"id":"C","text":"To produce the highest antibiotic level only after wound closure","explanation":"Waiting until closure misses the purpose of exposure at incision."},{"id":"D","text":"To ensure antibiotic activity begins only once infection is confirmed","explanation":"Prophylaxis precedes established infection rather than waiting for confirmation."}],"point":"Explain prophylaxis timing through adequate tissue antibiotic levels at incision.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--cefazolin_tissue_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_tissue_timing"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-cefazolin","itemType":"medication","title":"CEFAZOLIN","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Shortly after CEFAZOLIN, a patient develops wheeze, urticaria and hypotension. Which response takes priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treat the nausea before reassessing the wheeze","explanation":"Airway and circulatory threats take priority."},{"id":"B","text":"Record a mild rash for review at discharge","explanation":"Wheeze and hypotension are not a mild isolated rash."},{"id":"C","text":"Slow administration and finish the prescribed dose","explanation":"Continued exposure can worsen a systemic reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Stop exposure and activate emergency anaphylaxis care","explanation":"Airway and circulatory compromise require an emergency response."}],"point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--7-day--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving CEFAZOLIN develops acute kidney injury. Which prescription detail most needs review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Interval against serum potassium alone","explanation":"Potassium does not quantify the renal clearance needed for this review."},{"id":"B","text":"Dose against the last normal creatinine only","explanation":"An old normal result does not establish current clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"Dose and interval against current renal function","explanation":"Reduced clearance can require regimen adjustment."},{"id":"D","text":"Dose against symptom relief alone","explanation":"Benefit does not exclude accumulation or a need for adjustment."}],"point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--7-day--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After antibiotic treatment, a patient reports frequent watery stools and abdominal cramps. What is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Ignore it unless the next antibiotic dose is due","explanation":"Assessment is driven by symptoms, not the dosing time."},{"id":"B","text":"Assess and report possible antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"Significant diarrhea requires evaluation, including C. difficile when indicated."},{"id":"C","text":"Assume the infection is resolving through the bowel","explanation":"Diarrhea does not establish treatment success."},{"id":"D","text":"Recommend an antidiarrheal without further assessment","explanation":"Symptom suppression may be inappropriate before evaluating colitis."}],"point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--7-day--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Why is giving surgical antibiotic prophylaxis only after wound closure a concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prophylaxis is intended to treat established osteomyelitis","explanation":"Treatment of established infection is a different purpose."},{"id":"B","text":"The antibiotic cannot circulate after surgery","explanation":"It can circulate, but that misses the intended timing."},{"id":"C","text":"The drug becomes inactive when skin is closed","explanation":"Skin closure does not determine drug activity."},{"id":"D","text":"Protective tissue exposure was needed at incision","explanation":"Prophylaxis aims to cover the contamination period."}],"point":"Perioperative antibiotic timing should provide tissue exposure at incision.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--7-day--prophylaxis_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::prophylaxis_timing"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After several doses of CEFAZOLIN, fever persists and the patient deteriorates. What should guide the next review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Clinical course, cultures and the authorized treatment plan","explanation":"These help assess diagnosis, susceptibility and response."},{"id":"B","text":"The white cell count in isolation","explanation":"A single laboratory value does not replace the full clinical review."},{"id":"C","text":"The fever response to an antipyretic alone","explanation":"Temporary fever relief does not prove antimicrobial effectiveness."},{"id":"D","text":"An old culture from a different infection","explanation":"It may not represent the current pathogen or susceptibility."}],"point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--7-day--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":5}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-cefazolin","title":"CEFAZOLIN INJECTION, POWDER, FOR SOLUTION [HENRY SCHEIN INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=922fb1a1-ad4c-4a12-8d5d-00fe13af18e8"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial.","mechanism":"Beta-lactam cephalosporin that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","use":"Highly relevant to orthopaedics for perioperative antibacterial prophylaxis and for treatment of susceptible bacterial infections when prescribed.","administration":"Parenteral product; preparation and IV administration depend on the exact vial/product and order. Do not memorize one dilution or rate across all cefazolin products. Adjusted dosing may be needed with renal impairment.","monitor":"• Verify beta-lactam allergy history and watch for hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis. • Review renal function and ordered dose/interval. • Monitor IV site and clinical response. • Watch for significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea/C. difficile features. • For prophylaxis, timing relative to surgery matters—follow the surgical protocol/order.","why":"Cefazolin inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis. In surgery, adequate tissue levels at the time of incision help reduce surgical-site infection risk.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Exact vial strength/formulation in the ward was not recorded; verify the vial, diluent, route and local order."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial.","id":"section-0-f02fbe3a70853711"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Beta-lactam cephalosporin that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","id":"section-1-5ff8fc6a43a3e42c"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Highly relevant to orthopaedics for perioperative antibacterial prophylaxis and for treatment of susceptible bacterial infections when prescribed.","id":"section-2-c769a22cf41316d8"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Parenteral product; preparation and IV administration depend on the exact vial/product and order. Do not memorize one dilution or rate across all cefazolin products. Adjusted dosing may be needed with renal impairment.","id":"section-3-f71b769025b1696c"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Verify beta-lactam allergy history and watch for hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis. • Review renal function and ordered dose/interval. • Monitor IV site and clinical response. • Watch for significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea/C. difficile features. • For prophylaxis, timing relative to surgery matters—follow the surgical protocol/order.","id":"section-4-7293dc59d973d282"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Cefazolin inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis. In surgery, adequate tissue levels at the time of incision help reduce surgical-site infection risk.","id":"section-5-7ad5c601afcf2210"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Exact vial strength/formulation in the ward was not recorded; verify the vial, diluent, route and local order.","id":"section-6-28cbcb06fbfc8737"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-tramadol","title":"TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard","itemId":"ibd2-tramadol","itemType":"medication","title":"TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Mu agonism and reuptake inhibition","explanation":"Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduces angiotensin-II formation","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Retains water in the colon","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Predominantly beta1-mediated increased contractility","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Opioid/monoamine analgesic","explanation":"Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Non-opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"D","text":"NSAID","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::drug-class"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replacement therapy for an adrenal crisis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Prescribed treatment of moderate-to-severe pain","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Moderate-to-severe pain when an opioid analgesic is prescribed; common relevance after fractures, major trauma or surgery."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"After TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation, which change requires the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New nausea with preserved alertness","explanation":"This is less immediately threatening than reduced responsiveness with abnormal breathing."},{"id":"B","text":"New reduced responsiveness and shallow breathing","explanation":"This suggests a threat to ventilation from opioid toxicity."},{"id":"C","text":"New constipation and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These matter but do not outrank the described ventilatory threat."},{"id":"D","text":"New itching without airway or BP change","explanation":"This requires assessment but is less urgent than ventilation impairment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize opioid-related sedation and respiratory depression.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--opioid_respiration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::opioid_respiration"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"After TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation, which syndrome best explains agitation, fever, hyperreflexia, and autonomic instability?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Opioid respiratory toxicity","explanation":"This more typically produces depressed breathing and consciousness."},{"id":"B","text":"Isolated antimuscarinic intolerance","explanation":"This does not best explain the hyperreflexic serotonergic pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Serotonin toxicity","explanation":"The combination of neuromuscular excitation and autonomic changes fits."},{"id":"D","text":"Vancomycin infusion intolerance","explanation":"This is a different administration-related reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize serotonin toxicity and review serotonergic combinations.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--serotonin","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::serotonin"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which new neurological event during TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation therapy needs the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An unchanged chronic headache","explanation":"The case does not describe a new neurological deterioration in this option."},{"id":"B","text":"Improving original symptoms","explanation":"Therapeutic improvement does not itself identify neurotoxicity."},{"id":"C","text":"Mild unchanged nausea","explanation":"This is less urgent than a new seizure."},{"id":"D","text":"A new generalized seizure","explanation":"This is a potentially serious neurological adverse effect or another emergency."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient taking TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation is newly drowsy and ataxic but has stable vital signs. Which activity most directly requires additional assistance?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Using the call bell while seated with support","explanation":"This does not require the impaired balance involved in standing."},{"id":"B","text":"Choosing a meal while seated with support","explanation":"This does not carry the same immediate fall exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"Standing and transferring to the bathroom","explanation":"Impaired alertness and coordination increase fall risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Reporting pain while seated with support","explanation":"The described impairment most directly compromises unsupported mobility."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"An immobilized patient receiving TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation develops reduced stool frequency and straining. Which problem best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Constipation","explanation":"The symptoms fit impaired bowel transit associated with opioid exposure and immobility."},{"id":"B","text":"Serotonin toxicity","explanation":"The case does not describe autonomic and neuromuscular excitation."},{"id":"C","text":"Upper gastrointestinal bleeding","explanation":"No bleeding pattern is described."},{"id":"D","text":"Acute infusion intolerance","explanation":"This is not an infusion-related presentation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Opioids can contribute to constipation and require bowel-pattern review.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--constipation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::constipation"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which paired reassessment directly measures the intended analgesic response to TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Alertness and respiratory effort during rest","explanation":"These assess important adverse effects rather than analgesic benefit itself."},{"id":"B","text":"Pain intensity and ability to perform the agreed activity","explanation":"These compare pain and function with baseline."},{"id":"C","text":"BP and pulse during a postural change","explanation":"These assess hemodynamic tolerance rather than the pain response."},{"id":"D","text":"Stool frequency and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These assess bowel effects rather than analgesic benefit."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::pain_response"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Tramadol inhibits the reuptake of which two neurotransmitters?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dopamine and acetylcholine","explanation":"Neither is the pair for tramadol’s reuptake inhibition."},{"id":"B","text":"Dopamine and norepinephrine","explanation":"Norepinephrine fits, but dopamine is not the other named pathway."},{"id":"C","text":"Serotonin and norepinephrine","explanation":"Both are named in the mechanism and contribute to the analgesic/interaction profile."},{"id":"D","text":"Serotonin and acetylcholine","explanation":"Serotonin fits, but acetylcholine is not the second named pathway."}],"point":"Identify serotonin and norepinephrine as the reuptake pathways taught for tramadol.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--tramadol_monoamines","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::tramadol_monoamines"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pair of medicines can increase sedation and respiratory depression when combined with tramadol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Ibuprofen and meropenem","explanation":"These are not the highlighted sedating pair; meropenem has a different important interaction with valproate."},{"id":"B","text":"Losartan and paracetamol","explanation":"These are not the highlighted sedating pair."},{"id":"C","text":"Paracetamol and cefazolin","explanation":"These are not the sedating pair highlighted."},{"id":"D","text":"Gabapentin and promethazine","explanation":"Both are specifically named as potentially increasing CNS/respiratory depression with tramadol."}],"point":"Recognize additive sedation/respiratory risk with gabapentin or promethazine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--tramadol_named_sedatives","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::tramadol_named_sedatives"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient starts tramadol. Which new symptom is a recognized adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Gingival enlargement","explanation":"This is not the tramadol adverse effect listed."},{"id":"B","text":"Nausea","explanation":"Nausea is explicitly listed alongside dizziness and constipation."},{"id":"C","text":"Peripheral ankle edema","explanation":"This is not the tramadol adverse effect listed."},{"id":"D","text":"Persistent dry cough","explanation":"This is more associated with ACE-inhibitor treatment and is not applicable here for tramadol."}],"point":"Recognize nausea as an adverse effect taught for tramadol.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--tramadol_nausea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::tramadol_nausea"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":12},{"id":"ibd2-tramadol--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-tramadol","itemType":"medication","title":"TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient receiving TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation becomes difficult to rouse with slow, shallow respirations after a benzodiazepine. Which mechanism best explains the combined hazard?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Additive central nervous system and respiratory depression","explanation":"Both drug effects can impair ventilation and consciousness."},{"id":"B","text":"Accelerated opioid metabolism through renal filtration","explanation":"This does not explain increased toxicity after another sedative."},{"id":"C","text":"Enhanced serotonin release at motor endplates","explanation":"This does not describe the central depressant interaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Competitive reduction of opioid absorption","explanation":"This would not explain intensified central depression."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize opioid-related sedation and respiratory depression.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--advanced--opioid_respiration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::opioid_respiration"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient taking TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation and a serotonergic antidepressant becomes agitated and febrile. Which additional finding most strengthens concern for serotonin toxicity?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated ankle swelling","explanation":"This does not support the characteristic toxidrome."},{"id":"B","text":"Isolated postural BP reduction","explanation":"This is less specific for serotonin toxicity."},{"id":"C","text":"Markedly depressed respiratory effort","explanation":"This favors a central-depressant pattern instead."},{"id":"D","text":"Hyperreflexia","explanation":"Neuromuscular excitation supports the syndrome described in this case."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize serotonin toxicity and review serotonergic combinations.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--advanced--serotonin","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::serotonin"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A new seizure occurs during TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation therapy. Which review most directly tests a drug-exposure explanation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Recent stool pattern and the prescribed bowel regimen","explanation":"These do not address the principal drug-neurotoxicity explanation."},{"id":"B","text":"Recent dose, renal/hepatic clearance, and interacting medicines","explanation":"Exposure changes can increase neurotoxicity or alter seizure protection."},{"id":"C","text":"Recent pain scores and the effectiveness of non-drug comfort measures","explanation":"These do not quantify the relevant drug exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Recent wound appearance and the frequency of dressing changes","explanation":"These may inform infection but not the direct exposure hypothesis."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--advanced--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"An older patient taking TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation has a normal SpO2 but is newly drowsy and sways during transfers. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Transfer safety is impaired despite preserved oxygenation","explanation":"SpO2 does not measure balance or alertness."},{"id":"B","text":"Respiratory failure is established by the swaying","explanation":"Unsteadiness alone does not establish respiratory failure."},{"id":"C","text":"A new focal stroke is established by the drowsiness","explanation":"The findings require assessment but are not diagnostic of a focal stroke."},{"id":"D","text":"Improved drug efficacy is established by the drowsiness","explanation":"An adverse effect does not establish therapeutic success."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--advanced--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A fracture patient taking TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation has less pain but worsening constipation during bed rest. Which causal explanation best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Medication-related bowel acceleration combined with immobility","explanation":"Opioid effects generally slow rather than accelerate transit."},{"id":"B","text":"Medication-related bowel slowing combined with immobility","explanation":"The two factors can act together."},{"id":"C","text":"Improved intestinal perfusion caused by pain relief","explanation":"This does not explain the constipation pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced analgesic absorption caused by improved mobility","explanation":"The case describes immobility and improved analgesia."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Opioids can contribute to constipation and require bowel-pattern review.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--advanced--constipation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::constipation"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"The prescribed TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."},{"id":"B","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."},{"id":"C","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."},{"id":"D","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--advanced--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::formulation"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-tramadol","itemType":"medication","title":"TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient given TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation is difficult to wake and breathing slowly. What is the priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Walk the patient to improve alertness","explanation":"Mobilization is unsafe and does not support ventilation."},{"id":"B","text":"Support airway and breathing and summon urgent help","explanation":"Sedation with slow breathing suggests dangerous respiratory depression."},{"id":"C","text":"Let the patient sleep and repeat observations later","explanation":"Delay can allow respiratory failure to progress."},{"id":"D","text":"Reassess the pain score before calling for help","explanation":"Ventilation takes priority over analgesic benefit."}],"point":"Recognize opioid-related sedation and respiratory depression.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day--opioid_respiration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::opioid_respiration"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient on TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation and a serotonergic antidepressant develops fever, agitation and clonus. What needs urgent review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Routine postural hypotension","explanation":"Postural hypotension does not account for clonus and fever."},{"id":"B","text":"Expected isolated opioid constipation","explanation":"Constipation does not explain fever and clonus."},{"id":"C","text":"An uncomplicated local infusion reaction","explanation":"A local reaction does not explain the systemic neurological pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Possible serotonin toxicity","explanation":"The medication combination and neuromuscular findings support this concern."}],"point":"Recognize serotonin toxicity and review serotonergic combinations.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day--serotonin","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::serotonin"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A seizure occurs after a medicine change involving TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation. Which review best addresses a medication contribution?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The previous seizure history without current exposure","explanation":"History matters but does not adequately assess a new medication contribution."},{"id":"B","text":"The most recent dose time without the regimen","explanation":"One timestamp does not establish exposure over time."},{"id":"C","text":"Recent doses, renal trend and interacting medicines","explanation":"These can reveal accumulation or an interaction."},{"id":"D","text":"The original admission creatinine alone","explanation":"Renal function may have changed during treatment."}],"point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A newly drowsy patient receiving TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation wants to walk alone. What is the best immediate plan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the previous day's mobility assessment","explanation":"A new change requires current assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"Assist mobility and reassess sedation","explanation":"New drowsiness makes unassisted walking unsafe."},{"id":"C","text":"Allow walking because oxygen saturation is normal","explanation":"Normal SpO2 does not exclude impaired balance or sedation."},{"id":"D","text":"Delay help until the next scheduled observation","explanation":"The current fall risk needs action now."}],"point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Pain is controlled with TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation, but stools have become hard and infrequent. Which response is appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Review bowel pattern and the prescribed prevention plan","explanation":"Opioid-related reduced motility can need bowel management."},{"id":"B","text":"Reassure that reduced mobility prevents impaction","explanation":"Immobility can worsen constipation rather than prevent it."},{"id":"C","text":"Stop checking bowel function once pain improves","explanation":"Analgesic benefit does not remove bowel adverse effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Treat absent stools as proof of drug absorption","explanation":"Bowel inactivity does not measure absorption."}],"point":"Opioids can contribute to constipation and require bowel-pattern review.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day--constipation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::constipation"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A supplied TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation product has a different route or release type from the prescription. What is the safest action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the previous patient's preparation instructions","explanation":"Preparation must match this exact product and order."},{"id":"B","text":"Crush the product to make absorption equivalent","explanation":"Crushing can alter release and may be unsafe."},{"id":"C","text":"Verify the exact product and authorized substitution","explanation":"Matching a drug name does not establish formulation equivalence."},{"id":"D","text":"Substitute if the tablet strength looks similar","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish release or route suitability."}],"point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::formulation"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which note best documents benefit after prescribed TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation for pain?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The patient swallowed the medicine without difficulty","explanation":"Swallowing does not establish analgesic response."},{"id":"B","text":"The scheduled dose was administered at 10:00","explanation":"Administration is not evidence of benefit."},{"id":"C","text":"The medication chart contains a signed entry","explanation":"A signature confirms administration, not effectiveness."},{"id":"D","text":"Pain fell from 7 to 3 and movement became easier","explanation":"This records symptom and functional response."}],"point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--7-day--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::pain_response"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":7}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-tramadol","title":"TRAMADOL HYDROCHLORIDE TABLET, EXTENDED RELEASE [PREFERRED PHARMACEUTICALS, INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=83367367-5654-4078-831a-69ade48bc795"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects.","mechanism":"Weak mu-opioid receptor agonism plus inhibition of serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake.","use":"Moderate-to-severe pain when an opioid analgesic is prescribed; common relevance after fractures, major trauma or surgery.","administration":"For the oral capsule, give PO exactly as ordered and confirm formulation/strength. Do not assume oral and parenteral products are interchangeable. Avoid unverified route conversion. Dose selection can require adjustment for renal/hepatic impairment and interacting medicines.","monitor":"• Assess pain, respiratory rate/effort, sedation/level of consciousness and fall risk. • Serious respiratory depression can occur, especially with other CNS depressants. • Tramadol can cause seizures and serotonin syndrome. • Watch for nausea, dizziness and constipation. • Combining with gabapentin or sedating promethazine can increase sedation/respiratory risk. • Escalate excessive sedation, slow/shallow breathing or reduced responsiveness.","why":"Opioid activity provides analgesia, while monoamine effects contribute to both analgesia and interaction risks such as serotonin syndrome and seizures.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026, including an oral capsule. Other ward formulations may differ; verify the exact product, strength and route before administration."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects.","id":"section-0-e8332608edc23353"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Weak mu-opioid receptor agonism plus inhibition of serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake.","id":"section-1-346c86f53770937b"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Moderate-to-severe pain when an opioid analgesic is prescribed; common relevance after fractures, major trauma or surgery.","id":"section-2-4e1d69f5e663d5d7"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"For the oral capsule, give PO exactly as ordered and confirm formulation/strength. Do not assume oral and parenteral products are interchangeable. Avoid unverified route conversion. Dose selection can require adjustment for renal/hepatic impairment and interacting medicines.","id":"section-3-13b0acd2fd96e776"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Assess pain, respiratory rate/effort, sedation/level of consciousness and fall risk. • Serious respiratory depression can occur, especially with other CNS depressants. • Tramadol can cause seizures and serotonin syndrome. • Watch for nausea, dizziness and constipation. • Combining with gabapentin or sedating promethazine can increase sedation/respiratory risk. • Escalate excessive sedation, slow/shallow breathing or reduced responsiveness.","id":"section-4-5ca01be119c163f7"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Opioid activity provides analgesia, while monoamine effects contribute to both analgesia and interaction risks such as serotonin syndrome and seizures.","id":"section-5-2a38b5def33cade3"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026, including an oral capsule. Other ward formulations may differ; verify the exact product, strength and route before administration.","id":"section-6-b070a61de672e856"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-meropenem","title":"MEROPENEM","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard","itemId":"ibd2-meropenem","itemType":"medication","title":"MEROPENEM","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of MEROPENEM?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose-dependent dopaminergic and adrenergic effects","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"B","text":"Antagonizes NMDA receptors","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"C","text":"Activates plasminogen to break down fibrin","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does MEROPENEM belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Carbapenem beta-lactam","explanation":"Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"B","text":"Glycopeptide","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"C","text":"Aminoglycoside","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"D","text":"Beta-lactam","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::drug-class"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of MEROPENEM?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Relief of bronchospasm by beta2 stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Treatment of serious susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Serious susceptible bacterial infections. Current labeling includes complicated skin/skin-structure infection; it should not be treated as routine orthopaedic prophylaxis simply because it is broad spectrum."},{"id":"D","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Minutes after starting MEROPENEM, a patient develops wheeze, facial swelling, and hypotension. Which reaction best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Delayed marrow suppression","explanation":"This does not fit the rapid onset or the described manifestations."},{"id":"B","text":"Antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"This does not explain the immediate airway and circulatory pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Isolated infusion-site phlebitis","explanation":"A local vein reaction does not explain the multisystem findings."},{"id":"D","text":"Anaphylaxis","explanation":"Acute airway and circulatory compromise after exposure is strongly concerning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which result best informs assessment of accumulation risk and possible regimen adjustment for MEROPENEM?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"TSH trend and free thyroxine","explanation":"These assess thyroid function rather than renal elimination."},{"id":"B","text":"Creatinine trend and estimated GFR","explanation":"These inform renal clearance and regimen review."},{"id":"C","text":"ALT trend and serum bilirubin","explanation":"These assess hepatic injury rather than directly estimate renal clearance."},{"id":"D","text":"INR trend and prothrombin time","explanation":"These assess a coagulation pathway rather than renal clearance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"During MEROPENEM therapy, which new bowel pattern most warrants assessment for antibiotic-associated colitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Frequent, persistent watery diarrhea","explanation":"Persistent diarrhea during antibiotic exposure needs assessment for C. difficile and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced stool frequency during immobility","explanation":"This does not identify the complication being tested."},{"id":"C","text":"Hard stool with straining","explanation":"This points toward constipation rather than colitis-related diarrhea."},{"id":"D","text":"Normal formed stool after breakfast","explanation":"This is not a new concerning diarrhea pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which new neurological event during MEROPENEM therapy needs the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An unchanged chronic headache","explanation":"The case does not describe a new neurological deterioration in this option."},{"id":"B","text":"Mild unchanged nausea","explanation":"This is less urgent than a new seizure."},{"id":"C","text":"A new generalized seizure","explanation":"This is a potentially serious neurological adverse effect or another emergency."},{"id":"D","text":"Improving original symptoms","explanation":"Therapeutic improvement does not itself identify neurotoxicity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A bacterial infection is being treated with MEROPENEM. Which paired findings most directly inform whether the antimicrobial plan remains suitable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Appetite and bowel frequency","explanation":"These inform general tolerance but not the organism-directed treatment decision."},{"id":"B","text":"Infection response and culture susceptibility","explanation":"Clinical progress and organism susceptibility inform appropriateness."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain response and analgesic requirement","explanation":"These can improve without establishing antimicrobial susceptibility."},{"id":"D","text":"Fluid balance and serum sodium","explanation":"These inform hydration but do not establish antimicrobial appropriateness."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which information should be verified before preparing and administering IV meropenem?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The usual broad-spectrum antibiotic dilution and adult schedule","explanation":"Broad-spectrum classification does not establish product-specific preparation or dosing."},{"id":"B","text":"The ward’s usual cefazolin method and the same milligram dose","explanation":"A different antibiotic’s preparation cannot establish the meropenem method."},{"id":"C","text":"The supplied product instructions and the renal-reviewed order","explanation":"These establish the appropriate preparation and prescribed regimen for this patient."},{"id":"D","text":"The last meropenem patient’s preparation and dosing interval","explanation":"Another patient may have had a different product, indication, and renal function."}],"point":"Verify the meropenem IV product, indication, renal function, and order before preparation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--meropenem_iv_preparation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_iv_preparation"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":9},{"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-meropenem","itemType":"medication","title":"MEROPENEM","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"Before MEROPENEM, which prior antibiotic reaction carries the strongest immediate hypersensitivity concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Rapid wheeze and collapse after a beta-lactam","explanation":"The timing and cardiorespiratory involvement suggest a serious immediate reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Metallic taste during metronidazole therapy","explanation":"This is a different, commonly nonallergic adverse effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Diarrhea several days into clindamycin therapy","explanation":"This needs review but is not the same immediate reaction pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Local IV discomfort without systemic symptoms","explanation":"This is less specific for a serious immediate systemic allergy."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Renal clearance falls during MEROPENEM therapy while the dose and interval remain unchanged. Which pharmacokinetic change is most concerning?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A shorter elimination half-life","explanation":"Reduced clearance tends to prolong, not shorten, elimination."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased drug accumulation","explanation":"Reduced renal elimination can raise exposure between doses."},{"id":"C","text":"Decreased drug accumulation","explanation":"This is the opposite of the expected effect of reduced clearance."},{"id":"D","text":"A lower steady-state exposure","explanation":"With unchanged dosing, reduced clearance can increase exposure."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient develops persistent watery diarrhea after MEROPENEM. Which explanation should be considered during assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An immediate IgE-mediated airway reaction","explanation":"The case describes a gastrointestinal pattern rather than acute anaphylaxis."},{"id":"B","text":"Therapeutic stimulation of intestinal glucose absorption","explanation":"This does not explain the antibiotic-associated diarrhea warning."},{"id":"C","text":"Antibiotic-associated disturbance of intestinal flora","explanation":"This can permit C. difficile-associated disease."},{"id":"D","text":"An intended increase in osmotic laxative activity","explanation":"The antibiotic is not being used as an osmotic laxative."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A new seizure occurs during MEROPENEM therapy. Which review most directly tests a drug-exposure explanation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Recent dose, renal/hepatic clearance, and interacting medicines","explanation":"Exposure changes can increase neurotoxicity or alter seizure protection."},{"id":"B","text":"Recent wound appearance and the frequency of dressing changes","explanation":"These may inform infection but not the direct exposure hypothesis."},{"id":"C","text":"Recent pain scores and the effectiveness of non-drug comfort measures","explanation":"These do not quantify the relevant drug exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Recent stool pattern and the prescribed bowel regimen","explanation":"These do not address the principal drug-neurotoxicity explanation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient previously controlled on valproate has breakthrough seizures after meropenem starts. Which interaction best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increased valproate concentrations","explanation":"This reverses the characteristic carbapenem interaction described."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced valproate absorption caused by iron chelation","explanation":"The important interaction here is not the fluoroquinolone/mineral interaction."},{"id":"C","text":"Direct conversion of valproate into meropenem","explanation":"The drugs do not interconvert in this way."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced valproate concentrations","explanation":"Carbapenems can markedly lower valproate exposure."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Meropenem can lower valproate exposure and cause breakthrough seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced--valproate","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::valproate"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Meropenem is available on the orthopaedic ward. Is broad-spectrum activity alone a sufficient reason to use it as routine perioperative prophylaxis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"No; meropenem has no activity in skin or soft-tissue infections","explanation":"The conclusion about routine prophylaxis is right, but this rationale is false; meropenem can treat susceptible skin/skin-structure infections."},{"id":"B","text":"Yes; ward stock establishes the intended prophylactic indication","explanation":"Stock or observation does not establish the purpose of an individual prescription."},{"id":"C","text":"Yes; broader activity makes it preferable for every operation","explanation":"Broader coverage is not a universal reason to replace the indicated prophylactic regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"No; the prescribed indication and antimicrobial plan must support its use","explanation":"Ward availability and broad activity do not establish an appropriate prophylactic indication."}],"point":"Do not treat meropenem as routine orthopaedic prophylaxis solely because of broad coverage.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced--meropenem_not_prophylaxis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_not_prophylaxis"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with preserved renal function is prescribed meropenem. Which history still increases concern about seizure risk?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Previous opioid-related constipation","explanation":"This does not establish the CNS risk factor asked about."},{"id":"B","text":"Previous seizure disorder","explanation":"A seizure disorder is CNS disease and increases concern about seizures even with preserved renal function."},{"id":"C","text":"Previous nitrate-related headache","explanation":"This does not establish the CNS disease or seizure history asked about."},{"id":"D","text":"Previous NSAID-related GI bleeding","explanation":"This warrants medication review but does not establish the CNS risk factor asked about."}],"point":"Include pre-existing CNS disease when reviewing meropenem-related seizure risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced--meropenem_cns_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_cns_risk"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":7},{"id":"ibd2-meropenem--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-meropenem","itemType":"medication","title":"MEROPENEM","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Shortly after MEROPENEM, a patient develops wheeze, urticaria and hypotension. Which response takes priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treat the nausea before reassessing the wheeze","explanation":"Airway and circulatory threats take priority."},{"id":"B","text":"Slow administration and finish the prescribed dose","explanation":"Continued exposure can worsen a systemic reaction."},{"id":"C","text":"Stop exposure and activate emergency anaphylaxis care","explanation":"Airway and circulatory compromise require an emergency response."},{"id":"D","text":"Record a mild rash for review at discharge","explanation":"Wheeze and hypotension are not a mild isolated rash."}],"point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--7-day--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving MEROPENEM develops acute kidney injury. Which prescription detail most needs review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose and interval against current renal function","explanation":"Reduced clearance can require regimen adjustment."},{"id":"B","text":"Dose against the last normal creatinine only","explanation":"An old normal result does not establish current clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"Dose against symptom relief alone","explanation":"Benefit does not exclude accumulation or a need for adjustment."},{"id":"D","text":"Interval against serum potassium alone","explanation":"Potassium does not quantify the renal clearance needed for this review."}],"point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--7-day--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After antibiotic treatment, a patient reports frequent watery stools and abdominal cramps. What is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Assume the infection is resolving through the bowel","explanation":"Diarrhea does not establish treatment success."},{"id":"B","text":"Ignore it unless the next antibiotic dose is due","explanation":"Assessment is driven by symptoms, not the dosing time."},{"id":"C","text":"Recommend an antidiarrheal without further assessment","explanation":"Symptom suppression may be inappropriate before evaluating colitis."},{"id":"D","text":"Assess and report possible antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"Significant diarrhea requires evaluation, including C. difficile when indicated."}],"point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--7-day--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A seizure occurs after a medicine change involving MEROPENEM. Which review best addresses a medication contribution?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The previous seizure history without current exposure","explanation":"History matters but does not adequately assess a new medication contribution."},{"id":"B","text":"Recent doses, renal trend and interacting medicines","explanation":"These can reveal accumulation or an interaction."},{"id":"C","text":"The most recent dose time without the regimen","explanation":"One timestamp does not establish exposure over time."},{"id":"D","text":"The original admission creatinine alone","explanation":"Renal function may have changed during treatment."}],"point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--7-day--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Why might a previously controlled epilepsy patient seize after meropenem is started with valproate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Meropenem reliably increases valproate concentrations","explanation":"The important interaction lowers valproate exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"Meropenem can reduce valproate concentrations","explanation":"This interaction can cause loss of seizure control."},{"id":"C","text":"The combination cannot affect anticonvulsant treatment","explanation":"Carbapenem-valproate interactions are clinically important."},{"id":"D","text":"Valproate prevents every antibiotic-related seizure","explanation":"It does not eliminate interactions or all seizure risks."}],"point":"Meropenem can lower valproate exposure and cause breakthrough seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--7-day--valproate","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::valproate"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After several doses of MEROPENEM, fever persists and the patient deteriorates. What should guide the next review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The fever response to an antipyretic alone","explanation":"Temporary fever relief does not prove antimicrobial effectiveness."},{"id":"B","text":"The white cell count in isolation","explanation":"A single laboratory value does not replace the full clinical review."},{"id":"C","text":"Clinical course, cultures and the authorized treatment plan","explanation":"These help assess diagnosis, susceptibility and response."},{"id":"D","text":"An old culture from a different infection","explanation":"It may not represent the current pathogen or susceptibility."}],"point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--7-day--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":6}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-meropenem","title":"MEROPENEM INJECTION, POWDER, FOR SOLUTION [WG CRITICAL CARE, LLC]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=74ff19b5-6156-4a27-affb-744b0ce1aeec"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial.","mechanism":"Carbapenem beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","use":"Serious susceptible bacterial infections. Current labeling includes complicated skin/skin-structure infection; it should not be treated as routine orthopaedic prophylaxis simply because it is broad spectrum.","administration":"IV. Reconstitution, dilution, infusion method and dose depend on the exact product, indication and renal function. Current labeling requires dose adjustment when renal function is reduced.","monitor":"• Check beta-lactam allergy history and monitor for hypersensitivity. • Review renal function because accumulation increases adverse-effect risk. • Watch for seizures/neurologic changes, particularly with renal impairment or CNS disease. • Monitor for significant diarrhea/C. difficile. • IMPORTANT interaction: meropenem can markedly lower valproic acid/divalproex concentrations and trigger breakthrough seizures.","why":"Meropenem inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis and has broad activity, but broad coverage does not replace culture-guided therapy and antimicrobial stewardship.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED repeatedly in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Exact vial strength was not recorded; verify the vial and order."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial.","id":"section-0-172212d9f00e3bc8"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Carbapenem beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","id":"section-1-a54bcc99c1436284"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Serious susceptible bacterial infections. Current labeling includes complicated skin/skin-structure infection; it should not be treated as routine orthopaedic prophylaxis simply because it is broad spectrum.","id":"section-2-cf20ecce68f2e7c1"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"IV. Reconstitution, dilution, infusion method and dose depend on the exact product, indication and renal function. Current labeling requires dose adjustment when renal function is reduced.","id":"section-3-3f245b647a1e82a2"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Check beta-lactam allergy history and monitor for hypersensitivity. • Review renal function because accumulation increases adverse-effect risk. • Watch for seizures/neurologic changes, particularly with renal impairment or CNS disease. • Monitor for significant diarrhea/C. difficile. • IMPORTANT interaction: meropenem can markedly lower valproic acid/divalproex concentrations and trigger breakthrough seizures.","id":"section-4-df161e73a41a1c1d"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Meropenem inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis and has broad activity, but broad coverage does not replace culture-guided therapy and antimicrobial stewardship.","id":"section-5-db38ab732427baf1"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED repeatedly in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Exact vial strength was not recorded; verify the vial and order.","id":"section-6-fa56b9860ce40dee"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-prothiazine","title":"PROTHIAZINE — promethazine","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard","itemId":"ibd2-prothiazine","itemType":"medication","title":"PROTHIAZINE — promethazine","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of PROTHIAZINE — promethazine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Enhances GABA-A receptor activity","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Promethazine also has anticholinergic effects and can add to opioid-related sedation."},{"id":"B","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis through PBPs","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Promethazine also has anticholinergic effects and can add to opioid-related sedation."},{"id":"C","text":"Sedating antihistamine/antiemetic effects","explanation":"Promethazine also has anticholinergic effects and can add to opioid-related sedation."},{"id":"D","text":"Slows AV-nodal calcium-dependent conduction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Promethazine also has anticholinergic effects and can add to opioid-related sedation."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::principal-action"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does PROTHIAZINE — promethazine belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Peripheral antimuscarinic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Sedating first-generation H1 antihistamine; phenothiazine antiemetic."},{"id":"B","text":"First-generation H1 antagonist","explanation":"Sedating first-generation H1 antihistamine; phenothiazine antiemetic."},{"id":"C","text":"D2 antagonist/prokinetic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Sedating first-generation H1 antihistamine; phenothiazine antiemetic."},{"id":"D","text":"Histamine analogue","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Sedating first-generation H1 antihistamine; phenothiazine antiemetic."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::drug-class"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of PROTHIAZINE — promethazine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Prescribed control of nausea or allergic symptoms","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: May be used for nausea/vomiting, allergic symptoms or sedation depending on the order. In postoperative patients, its sedating effect is clinically important."},{"id":"D","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient taking PROTHIAZINE — promethazine is newly drowsy and ataxic but has stable vital signs. Which activity most directly requires additional assistance?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Standing and transferring to the bathroom","explanation":"Impaired alertness and coordination increase fall risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Using the call bell while seated with support","explanation":"This does not require the impaired balance involved in standing."},{"id":"C","text":"Choosing a meal while seated with support","explanation":"This does not carry the same immediate fall exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Reporting pain while seated with support","explanation":"The described impairment most directly compromises unsupported mobility."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which pair of effects is consistent with the antimuscarinic component of PROTHIAZINE — promethazine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Miosis and bronchial secretions","explanation":"These do not fit the principal antimuscarinic pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Dry mouth and urinary retention","explanation":"Muscarinic blockade can reduce secretions and impair bladder emptying."},{"id":"C","text":"Salivation and urinary urgency","explanation":"These are opposite to the characteristic antimuscarinic effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Diarrhea and increased lacrimation","explanation":"These suggest increased rather than blocked muscarinic activity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize muscarinic-blockade adverse effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard--anticholinergic","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::anticholinergic"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which receptor action explains promethazine’s antihistamine effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Beta2-receptor stimulation","explanation":"This produces bronchodilation rather than the antihistamine action."},{"id":"B","text":"Muscarinic receptor stimulation","explanation":"Promethazine has anticholinergic activity, not muscarinic stimulation."},{"id":"C","text":"Histamine H2 blockade","explanation":"H2 blockade is associated with gastric acid suppression, not this antihistamine action."},{"id":"D","text":"Histamine H1 blockade","explanation":"H1 blockade is the antihistamine action described."}],"point":"Identify H1-receptor blockade as promethazine’s taught antihistamine action.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard--promethazine_h1","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::promethazine_h1"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking promethazine reports dizziness specifically on standing. Which assessment most directly checks the suspected adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Postural BP with associated symptoms","explanation":"Orthostatic hypotension is explicitly listed and can contribute to falls."},{"id":"B","text":"Hearing acuity with ear symptoms","explanation":"These do not directly assess the named circulatory adverse effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Pupil size with response to light","explanation":"This does not directly assess the suspected postural BP change."},{"id":"D","text":"Bowel sounds with stool frequency","explanation":"These do not establish the cause of positional dizziness."}],"point":"Assess postural hypotension associated with promethazine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--standard--promethazine_postural_bp","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::promethazine_postural_bp"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":7},{"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-prothiazine","itemType":"medication","title":"PROTHIAZINE — promethazine","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"An older patient taking PROTHIAZINE — promethazine has a normal SpO2 but is newly drowsy and sways during transfers. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Respiratory failure is established by the swaying","explanation":"Unsteadiness alone does not establish respiratory failure."},{"id":"B","text":"A new focal stroke is established by the drowsiness","explanation":"The findings require assessment but are not diagnostic of a focal stroke."},{"id":"C","text":"Improved drug efficacy is established by the drowsiness","explanation":"An adverse effect does not establish therapeutic success."},{"id":"D","text":"Transfer safety is impaired despite preserved oxygenation","explanation":"SpO2 does not measure balance or alertness."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--advanced--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient taking PROTHIAZINE — promethazine also receives an opioid or another sedative. Which monitoring pair best addresses the interaction described?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bowel sounds and abdominal girth","explanation":"These do not directly assess the immediate central-depressant hazard."},{"id":"B","text":"Consciousness and respiratory effort","explanation":"Combined depressant effects can compromise alertness and ventilation."},{"id":"C","text":"Oral lesions and swallowing comfort","explanation":"These do not monitor the principal combined sedative effect."},{"id":"D","text":"Tendon tenderness and joint range","explanation":"These assess a different medication-related problem."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"CNS depressants can compound sedation and respiratory risk.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--advanced--cns_combination","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::cns_combination"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"The prescribed PROTHIAZINE — promethazine and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."},{"id":"B","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."},{"id":"C","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."},{"id":"D","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--advanced--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::formulation"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The ward has Prothiazine tablets, but a patient cannot take oral medicines. What should happen before changing the formulation or route?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Clarify an authorized alternative formulation and route","explanation":"The tablet brand does not establish an injectable product or an authorized route conversion."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the tablet strength as the equivalent injectable dose","explanation":"The observed tablet strength does not establish an injectable equivalent."},{"id":"C","text":"Dissolve the tablet and use the existing IV access","explanation":"An oral tablet is not a sterile injectable preparation."},{"id":"D","text":"Keep the same order and choose any promethazine injection","explanation":"A change in formulation/route requires an authorized, product-specific plan."}],"point":"Do not infer an injectable route from the observed oral promethazine brand.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--advanced--promethazine_route","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::promethazine_route"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-prothiazine","itemType":"medication","title":"PROTHIAZINE — promethazine","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A newly drowsy patient receiving PROTHIAZINE — promethazine wants to walk alone. What is the best immediate plan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Assist mobility and reassess sedation","explanation":"New drowsiness makes unassisted walking unsafe."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the previous day's mobility assessment","explanation":"A new change requires current assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Delay help until the next scheduled observation","explanation":"The current fall risk needs action now."},{"id":"D","text":"Allow walking because oxygen saturation is normal","explanation":"Normal SpO2 does not exclude impaired balance or sedation."}],"point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--7-day--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving PROTHIAZINE — promethazine develops dry mouth, blurred vision and difficulty passing urine. Which pattern fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A selective fall in blood glucose","explanation":"The symptom pattern does not establish hypoglycemia."},{"id":"B","text":"A therapeutic increase in bladder emptying","explanation":"Urinary retention is an adverse effect, not improved emptying."},{"id":"C","text":"An isolated local skin reaction","explanation":"Several organ systems are involved, not just skin."},{"id":"D","text":"Antimuscarinic adverse effects","explanation":"Muscarinic blockade can affect salivation, vision and bladder emptying."}],"point":"Recognize muscarinic-blockade adverse effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--7-day--anticholinergic","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::anticholinergic"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A new opioid is added to a sedating regimen containing PROTHIAZINE — promethazine. Which change needs close observation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An isolated mild rash without sedation","explanation":"This suggests a different adverse-effect pathway."},{"id":"B","text":"Hard stools without altered consciousness","explanation":"This suggests constipation rather than respiratory depression."},{"id":"C","text":"Increasing drowsiness with slow or shallow breathing","explanation":"These suggest additive CNS and respiratory depression."},{"id":"D","text":"Dry mouth with urinary hesitancy","explanation":"This more directly suggests antimuscarinic effects."}],"point":"CNS depressants can compound sedation and respiratory risk.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--7-day--cns_combination","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::cns_combination"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A supplied PROTHIAZINE — promethazine product has a different route or release type from the prescription. What is the safest action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Crush the product to make absorption equivalent","explanation":"Crushing can alter release and may be unsafe."},{"id":"B","text":"Verify the exact product and authorized substitution","explanation":"Matching a drug name does not establish formulation equivalence."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the previous patient's preparation instructions","explanation":"Preparation must match this exact product and order."},{"id":"D","text":"Substitute if the tablet strength looks similar","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish release or route suitability."}],"point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-prothiazine--7-day--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-prothiazine::formulation"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":4}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-prothiazine","title":"PROMETHAZINE HYDROCHLORIDE TABLET [DIRECT RX]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=bae9c9c1-107f-0fcb-e053-2a95a90a75b2"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Sedating first-generation H1 antihistamine; phenothiazine antiemetic.","mechanism":"Blocks histamine H1 receptors and also has anticholinergic and antidopaminergic effects.","use":"May be used for nausea/vomiting, allergic symptoms or sedation depending on the order. In postoperative patients, its sedating effect is clinically important.","administration":"For the ward tablet, PO as ordered. Do not infer an injectable route from the tablet brand. Check other sedating medicines before administration.","monitor":"• Watch drowsiness, dizziness, confusion and orthostatic hypotension/fall risk. • Promethazine has anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and urinary retention. • Sedation/respiratory depression can be worse with opioids such as tramadol or other CNS depressants. • Escalate excessive sedation or breathing impairment.","why":"H1 blockade provides antihistamine/antiemetic effects but also causes substantial sedation and anticholinergic effects.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Palestinian MoH lists Prothiazine 25 mg tablets containing promethazine HCl; verify the exact pack/strength in hand."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Sedating first-generation H1 antihistamine; phenothiazine antiemetic.","id":"section-0-c4140951ca2cd25d"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Blocks histamine H1 receptors and also has anticholinergic and antidopaminergic effects.","id":"section-1-c54fa33aa0241a45"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"May be used for nausea/vomiting, allergic symptoms or sedation depending on the order. In postoperative patients, its sedating effect is clinically important.","id":"section-2-cbe9f5dffa664044"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"For the ward tablet, PO as ordered. Do not infer an injectable route from the tablet brand. Check other sedating medicines before administration.","id":"section-3-06d7b27a00907620"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Watch drowsiness, dizziness, confusion and orthostatic hypotension/fall risk. • Promethazine has anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and urinary retention. • Sedation/respiratory depression can be worse with opioids such as tramadol or other CNS depressants. • Escalate excessive sedation or breathing impairment.","id":"section-4-67c8c55eb30b2730"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"H1 blockade provides antihistamine/antiemetic effects but also causes substantial sedation and anticholinergic effects.","id":"section-5-8aa06eddf68f60d7"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Palestinian MoH lists Prothiazine 25 mg tablets containing promethazine HCl; verify the exact pack/strength in hand.","id":"section-6-78e0913da6555339"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-gabapentin","title":"GABAPENTIN","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--standard","itemId":"ibd2-gabapentin","itemType":"medication","title":"GABAPENTIN","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of GABAPENTIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Relaxes arteriolar smooth muscle","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: This modulates excitatory neurotransmitter release; it is not a direct GABA agonist."},{"id":"B","text":"Binds the alpha-2-delta calcium-channel subunit","explanation":"This modulates excitatory neurotransmitter release; it is not a direct GABA agonist."},{"id":"C","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: This modulates excitatory neurotransmitter release; it is not a direct GABA agonist."},{"id":"D","text":"Modulates membrane stability and repolarization","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: This modulates excitatory neurotransmitter release; it is not a direct GABA agonist."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-gabapentin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does GABAPENTIN belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Gabapentinoid","explanation":"Gabapentinoid anticonvulsant; neuropathic-pain agent."},{"id":"B","text":"Tricyclic antidepressant","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Gabapentinoid anticonvulsant; neuropathic-pain agent."},{"id":"C","text":"Benzodiazepine","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Gabapentinoid anticonvulsant; neuropathic-pain agent."},{"id":"D","text":"Anticonvulsant","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Gabapentinoid anticonvulsant; neuropathic-pain agent."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-gabapentin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of GABAPENTIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibition of platelet aggregation after coronary stenting","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Rapid IV replacement of circulating glucose","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Treatment of a susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Neuropathic-pain or selected seizure treatment","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Neuropathic pain/nerve-related pain and seizure indications. In an orthopaedic/trauma setting it may be used when pain has a neuropathic component, but confirm the patient's actual indication."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-gabapentin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which result best informs assessment of accumulation risk and possible regimen adjustment for GABAPENTIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"ALT trend and serum bilirubin","explanation":"These assess hepatic injury rather than directly estimate renal clearance."},{"id":"B","text":"TSH trend and free thyroxine","explanation":"These assess thyroid function rather than renal elimination."},{"id":"C","text":"Creatinine trend and estimated GFR","explanation":"These inform renal clearance and regimen review."},{"id":"D","text":"INR trend and prothrombin time","explanation":"These assess a coagulation pathway rather than renal clearance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--standard--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-gabapentin::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient taking GABAPENTIN is newly drowsy and ataxic but has stable vital signs. Which activity most directly requires additional assistance?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reporting pain while seated with support","explanation":"The described impairment most directly compromises unsupported mobility."},{"id":"B","text":"Standing and transferring to the bathroom","explanation":"Impaired alertness and coordination increase fall risk."},{"id":"C","text":"Using the call bell while seated with support","explanation":"This does not require the impaired balance involved in standing."},{"id":"D","text":"Choosing a meal while seated with support","explanation":"This does not carry the same immediate fall exposure."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--standard--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-gabapentin::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-gabapentin","itemType":"medication","title":"GABAPENTIN","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"Renal clearance falls du