ative. Which monitoring pair best addresses the interaction described?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Consciousness and respiratory effort","explanation":"Combined depressant effects can compromise alertness and ventilation."},{"id":"B","text":"Tendon tenderness and joint range","explanation":"These assess a different medication-related problem."},{"id":"C","text":"Bowel sounds and abdominal girth","explanation":"These do not directly assess the immediate central-depressant hazard."},{"id":"D","text":"Oral lesions and swallowing comfort","explanation":"These do not monitor the principal combined sedative effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"CNS depressants can compound sedation and respiratory risk.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--advanced--cns_combination","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-gabapentin::cns_combination"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient takes chronic GABAPENTIN for epilepsy and has omitted several doses because seizures stopped. Which concern is most relevant?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A reduction in the medicine’s enzyme-inducing potential alone","explanation":"That does not address the immediate loss of established seizure treatment."},{"id":"B","text":"Loss of maintained seizure protection","explanation":"Control while treated does not establish that interruption is safe."},{"id":"C","text":"An immediate increase in renal drug clearance","explanation":"Dose omission does not itself establish a renal-clearance change."},{"id":"D","text":"An expected increase in anticonvulsant exposure","explanation":"Missed doses generally reduce exposure rather than increase it."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"An interruption of chronic anticonvulsant treatment needs an authorized plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-gabapentin--advanced--anticonvulsant_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-gabapentin::anticonvulsant_stop"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Ankle edema appears after GABAPENTIN; the patient also 