classified as: Osmotic laxative; non-absorbable disaccharide."},{"id":"B","text":"Osmotic laxative","explanation":"Osmotic laxative; non-absorbable disaccharide."},{"id":"C","text":"Proton-pump inhibitor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Osmotic laxative; non-absorbable disaccharide."},{"id":"D","text":"Peripheral antimuscarinic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Osmotic laxative; non-absorbable disaccharide."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::drug-class"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of AVILAC — lactulose?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treatment of constipation","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Constipation. In orthopaedic patients, constipation may be promoted by immobility and opioid analgesics. Lactulose is also used in hepatic encephalopathy for a different indication."},{"id":"B","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which finding best evaluates the intended effect of AVILAC — lactulose when prescribed for constipation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A lower resting heart rate","explanation":"This is not the main laxative response target."},{"id":"B","text":"A higher oxygen saturation","explanation":"This does not directly evaluate constipation treatment."},{"id":"C","text":"A more comfortable, appropriate bowel pattern","explanation":"The treatment target is stool passage without excessive effect."},{"id":"D","text":"A lower INR","explanation":"This is not a measure of the osmotic laxative response."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor bowel response to osmotic laxative therapy.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--standard--laxative_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::laxative_response"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ibd2-avilac--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-avilac","itemType":"medication","title":"AVILAC — lactulose","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"During AVILAC — lactulose therapy, a patient develops excessive fluid loss, thirst, and postural dizziness. Which paired concern is most relevant?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated gastric acid suppression and reflux","explanation":"These do not explain the hemodynamic change."},{"id":"B","text":"Volume depletion and electrolyte disturbance","explanation":"Excess losses can impair circulating volume and alter electrolytes."},{"id":"C","text":"Volume expansion and raised filling pressures","explanation":"The described loss pattern suggests the opposite direction."},{"id":"D","text":"Isolated platelet activation and thrombosis","explanation":"This does not best explain the symptoms after fluid loss."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Excess fluid loss can cause volume depletion and electrolyte disturbance.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--advanced--dehydration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::dehydration"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"One lactulose order treats constipation; another treats hepatic encephalopathy. Why should one patient’s regimen not simply be copied to the other?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The drug changes from a laxative to a systemic antibiotic","explanation":"Lactulose does not become an antibiotic."},{"id":"B","text":"The route changes from oral to IV for encephalopathy","explanation":"The product is oral; this is not the relevant distinction."},{"id":"C","text":"The indication changes the treatment goal and authorized titration","explanation":"The same medicine can be used with different indication-specific goals."},{"id":"D","text":"The drug changes from osmotic action to opioid antagonism","explanation":"This is not lactulose’s pharmacological distinction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Lactulose goals differ between constipation and hepatic encephalopathy.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--advanced--lactulose_goal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::lactulose_goal"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":2},{"id":"ibd2-avilac--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-avilac","itemType":"medication","title":"AVILAC — lactulose","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which change indicates the intended constipation response to lactulose?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A lower pain score without any bowel change","explanation":"Pain score alone does not establish laxative response."},{"id":"B","text":"No stool with increasing abdominal distension","explanation":"This does not demonstrate constipation relief."},{"id":"C","text":"Easier passage of a soft stool","explanation":"This measures the desired bowel effect."},{"id":"D","text":"Increasing watery diarrhea with thirst","explanation":"This can indicate excessive effect and dehydration."}],"point":"Monitor bowel response to osmotic laxative therapy.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--7-day--laxative_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::laxative_response"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving AVILAC — lactulose develops marked fluid loss and postural dizziness. What needs reassessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Volume status, BP and relevant electrolytes","explanation":"Losses can cause hypovolemia and electrolyte disturbance."},{"id":"B","text":"A single urine colour observation without vitals","explanation":"Colour cannot establish the degree of circulatory compromise."},{"id":"C","text":"The previous week's weight without a current assessment","explanation":"An old weight does not explain a new symptomatic loss."},{"id":"D","text":"The original symptom response without fluid balance","explanation":"Benefit does not exclude excessive losses."}],"point":"Excess fluid loss can cause volume depletion and electrolyte disturbance.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--7-day--dehydration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::dehydration"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Why should the nurse verify the indication before judging a lactulose regimen?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Diarrhea always proves optimal treatment","explanation":"Excess diarrhea can cause dehydration and electrolyte loss."},{"id":"B","text":"The indication does not affect reassessment","explanation":"Clinical response must be judged against the prescribed purpose."},{"id":"C","text":"Every indication uses an identical clinical target","explanation":"Indication-specific goals differ."},{"id":"D","text":"Constipation and hepatic encephalopathy have different goals","explanation":"The treatment target and assessment plan depend on indication."}],"point":"Lactulose goals differ between constipation and hepatic encephalopathy.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-avilac--7-day--lactulose_goal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-avilac::lactulose_goal"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-avilac","title":"LACTULOSE SOLUTION [AMERICAN HEALTH PACKAGING]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=8b79d0d6-b9e3-436e-bab7-01451b6f45cd"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Osmotic laxative; non-absorbable disaccharide.","mechanism":"Non-absorbable sugar metabolized by colonic bacteria; its osmotic effect retains water in the colon and promotes stool passage.","use":"Constipation. In orthopaedic patients, constipation may be promoted by immobility and opioid analgesics. Lactulose is also used in hepatic encephalopathy for a different indication.","administration":"PO syrup as ordered. Measure the ordered volume accurately. The dose/goal differs between ordinary constipation and hepatic encephalopathy, so do not transfer one regimen to the other.","monitor":"• Monitor bowel pattern and response. • Excessive effect can cause diarrhea, abdominal cramping, dehydration and electrolyte disturbance. • Reassess if constipation persists despite therapy or if severe abdominal symptoms occur.","why":"Lactulose remains in the bowel and draws water osmotically into the colon, softening stool and promoting bowel movements.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Avilac syrup contains lactulose 66.7 g/100 mL in current product information; verify the bottle in hand."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Osmotic laxative; non-absorbable disaccharide.","id":"section-0-ca2fb913df714f87"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Non-absorbable sugar metabolized by colonic bacteria; its osmotic effect retains water in the colon and promotes stool passage.","id":"section-1-7a28db0307f48c38"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Constipation. In orthopaedic patients, constipation may be promoted by immobility and opioid analgesics. Lactulose is also used in hepatic encephalopathy for a different indication.","id":"section-2-63202f4b22f5ebba"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"PO syrup as ordered. Measure the ordered volume accurately. The dose/goal differs between ordinary constipation and hepatic encephalopathy, so do not transfer one regimen to the other.","id":"section-3-831ebe707079c4ec"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Monitor bowel pattern and response. • Excessive effect can cause diarrhea, abdominal cramping, dehydration and electrolyte disturbance. • Reassess if constipation persists despite therapy or if severe abdominal symptoms occur.","id":"section-4-56807edf9f13c4eb"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Lactulose remains in the bowel and draws water osmotically into the colon, softening stool and promoting bowel movements.","id":"section-5-4e69be39e2c3c991"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Avilac syrup contains lactulose 66.7 g/100 mL in current product information; verify the bottle in hand.","id":"section-6-b223ea7a780ff181"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-carbamazepine","title":"TEGRETOL — carbamazepine","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard","itemId":"ibd2-carbamazepine","itemType":"medication","title":"TEGRETOL — carbamazepine","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of TEGRETOL — carbamazepine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Stabilizes inactivated voltage-gated sodium channels","explanation":"Carbamazepine and phenytoin limit repetitive neuronal firing through sodium-channel effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Vestibular symptom treatment","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Carbamazepine and phenytoin limit repetitive neuronal firing through sodium-channel effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Disrupts protein synthesis at the 30S subunit","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Carbamazepine and phenytoin limit repetitive neuronal firing through sodium-channel effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Blocks fast sodium channels","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Carbamazepine and phenytoin limit repetitive neuronal firing through sodium-channel effects."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does TEGRETOL — carbamazepine belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Tricyclic antidepressant","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Anticonvulsant; voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker; neuropathic-pain agent."},{"id":"B","text":"Gabapentinoid","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Anticonvulsant; voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker; neuropathic-pain agent."},{"id":"C","text":"Anticonvulsant","explanation":"Anticonvulsant; voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker; neuropathic-pain agent."},{"id":"D","text":"Dissociative anesthetic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Anticonvulsant; voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker; neuropathic-pain agent."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of TEGRETOL — carbamazepine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Rapid IV replacement of circulating glucose","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Treatment of a susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Seizure or selected neuropathic-pain treatment","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Seizure disorders and selected neuropathic-pain conditions such as trigeminal neuralgia. In an orthopaedic patient, confirm whether it is being continued for epilepsy or prescribed for a specific neuropathic-pain indication."},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibition of platelet aggregation after coronary stenting","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which new finding during TEGRETOL — carbamazepine therapy most strongly suggests a serious cutaneous drug reaction?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dry mouth without a rash","explanation":"This points toward a different adverse-effect pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Rash with mucosal involvement","explanation":"This raises concern for severe hypersensitivity, including SJS/TEN."},{"id":"C","text":"Constipation without a rash","explanation":"This does not identify a severe cutaneous reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Mild tremor without a rash","explanation":"This does not establish the skin and mucosal syndrome."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A new severe rash can indicate a serious anticonvulsant reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard--rash_serious","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::rash_serious"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which ordered investigation best checks the cytopenia risk described with TEGRETOL — carbamazepine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Coagulation profile","explanation":"Clotting times do not replace measurement of cell counts."},{"id":"B","text":"Complete blood count","explanation":"It measures relevant blood-cell lines, including platelets and white cells."},{"id":"C","text":"Renal function profile","explanation":"Renal function is a different monitoring domain."},{"id":"D","text":"Hepatic function profile","explanation":"Liver tests do not directly quantify the blood-cell lines."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Treatment can cause cytopenias; use the ordered CBC/platelet monitoring.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard--cbc","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::cbc"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which abnormality is particularly relevant when a patient taking TEGRETOL — carbamazepine develops confusion and weakness?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hyperphosphatemia","explanation":"Phosphate elevation is not the specific risk identified here."},{"id":"B","text":"Hypoalbuminemia","explanation":"Albumin changes do not establish the sodium-related complication."},{"id":"C","text":"Hypercalcemia","explanation":"This is a different electrolyte problem, not the characteristic treatment risk tested."},{"id":"D","text":"Hyponatremia","explanation":"Low sodium can cause neurological symptoms."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Hyponatremia can cause neurological symptoms during susceptible treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard--low_na","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::low_na"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient taking TEGRETOL — carbamazepine is newly drowsy and ataxic but has stable vital signs. Which activity most directly requires additional assistance?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Standing and transferring to the bathroom","explanation":"Impaired alertness and coordination increase fall risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Using the call bell while seated with support","explanation":"This does not require the impaired balance involved in standing."},{"id":"C","text":"Choosing a meal while seated with support","explanation":"This does not carry the same immediate fall exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Reporting pain while seated with support","explanation":"The described impairment most directly compromises unsupported mobility."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--standard--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":7},{"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-carbamazepine","itemType":"medication","title":"TEGRETOL — carbamazepine","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient taking TEGRETOL — carbamazepine develops a new rash and painful mouth lesions while seizures remain controlled. Which conclusion best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced absorption explains the mucosal lesions","explanation":"Reduced absorption does not explain this cutaneous reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Drug withdrawal explains the mucosal lesions","explanation":"No interruption is described, and the pattern raises a different concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Loss of seizure control explains the mucosal lesions","explanation":"The seizures remain controlled, and this does not explain the rash."},{"id":"D","text":"Therapeutic benefit and serious hypersensitivity may coexist","explanation":"Seizure control does not exclude a dangerous drug reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A new severe rash can indicate a serious anticonvulsant reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced--rash_serious","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::rash_serious"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"During TEGRETOL — carbamazepine therapy, platelets fall and bruising develops. Which change most directly explains the bleeding tendency?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increased gastric acid suppression","explanation":"Acid suppression does not account for the platelet trend."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced platelet availability","explanation":"Thrombocytopenia impairs primary hemostasis."},{"id":"C","text":"Increased platelet availability","explanation":"This does not explain bruising with a falling count."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced serum glucose availability","explanation":"Glucose concentration does not directly explain this platelet-related problem."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Treatment can cause cytopenias; use the ordered CBC/platelet monitoring.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced--cbc","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::cbc"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient becomes confused during TEGRETOL — carbamazepine therapy and sodium has fallen substantially. What makes a medication-related electrolyte problem plausible?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A stable sodium trend and improved alertness","explanation":"This describes improvement, not the deterioration reported."},{"id":"B","text":"A normal neurological examination and a stable sodium trend","explanation":"Neither part matches the case."},{"id":"C","text":"A compatible neurological change and a normal sodium trend","explanation":"This does not describe the documented fall."},{"id":"D","text":"A compatible neurological change and a relevant sodium trend","explanation":"The symptoms and adverse effect support assessment together."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Hyponatremia can cause neurological symptoms during susceptible treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced--low_na","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::low_na"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"An older patient taking TEGRETOL — carbamazepine has a normal SpO2 but is newly drowsy and sways during transfers. Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Transfer safety is impaired despite preserved oxygenation","explanation":"SpO2 does not measure balance or alertness."},{"id":"B","text":"Improved drug efficacy is established by the drowsiness","explanation":"An adverse effect does not establish therapeutic success."},{"id":"C","text":"Respiratory failure is established by the swaying","explanation":"Unsteadiness alone does not establish respiratory failure."},{"id":"D","text":"A new focal stroke is established by the drowsiness","explanation":"The findings require assessment but are not diagnostic of a focal stroke."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Why can adding carbamazepine alter the effectiveness of another medicine cleared by inducible hepatic enzymes?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"It blocks all renal filtration of the other medicine","explanation":"This is not the hepatic enzyme-induction mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"It directly supplies the other medicine’s active ingredient","explanation":"The drugs are not interchangeable sources of active ingredient."},{"id":"C","text":"It can increase enzyme-mediated metabolism","explanation":"Enzyme induction can lower exposure to affected concomitant medicines."},{"id":"D","text":"It binds all other medicines inside the gut","explanation":"This is not the general interaction mechanism described."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Carbamazepine can alter other medicines through enzyme induction.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced--enzyme_induction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::enzyme_induction"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient takes chronic TEGRETOL — carbamazepine for epilepsy and has omitted several doses because seizures stopped. Which concern is most relevant?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An expected increase in anticonvulsant exposure","explanation":"Missed doses generally reduce exposure rather than increase it."},{"id":"B","text":"A reduction in the medicine’s enzyme-inducing potential alone","explanation":"That does not address the immediate loss of established seizure treatment."},{"id":"C","text":"Loss of maintained seizure protection","explanation":"Control while treated does not establish that interruption is safe."},{"id":"D","text":"An immediate increase in renal drug clearance","explanation":"Dose omission does not itself establish a renal-clearance change."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"An interruption of chronic anticonvulsant treatment needs an authorized plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced--anticonvulsant_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::anticonvulsant_stop"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"The prescribed TEGRETOL — carbamazepine and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."},{"id":"B","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."},{"id":"C","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."},{"id":"D","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--advanced--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::formulation"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":7},{"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-carbamazepine","itemType":"medication","title":"TEGRETOL — carbamazepine","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient on TEGRETOL — carbamazepine develops widespread rash, fever and mouth ulcers. What should the nurse do?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treat it as simple dry skin without reporting","explanation":"Fever and mouth ulcers are inconsistent with simple dryness."},{"id":"B","text":"Seek urgent assessment for a serious drug reaction","explanation":"Mucosal involvement and systemic symptoms are red flags."},{"id":"C","text":"Apply moisturizer and review at the next visit","explanation":"This delays assessment of a potentially severe reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Give the next dose because seizure control is good","explanation":"Treatment benefit does not exclude serious hypersensitivity."}],"point":"A new severe rash can indicate a serious anticonvulsant reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--7-day--rash_serious","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::rash_serious"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During TEGRETOL — carbamazepine therapy, which new finding most supports checking the prescribed CBC urgently?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Unexpected bruising with gum bleeding","explanation":"These suggest a platelet or other hematological problem."},{"id":"B","text":"Postural dizziness with an isolated BP fall","explanation":"This more directly calls for circulatory assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Dry mouth with urinary hesitancy","explanation":"This more directly suggests an antimuscarinic effect."},{"id":"D","text":"Burning feet with persistent numbness","explanation":"This more directly suggests neuropathy."}],"point":"Treatment can cause cytopenias; use the ordered CBC/platelet monitoring.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--7-day--cbc","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::cbc"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving TEGRETOL — carbamazepine has new confusion and a low sodium result. What is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeat the dose before reviewing consciousness","explanation":"A symptomatic electrolyte problem needs prompt review."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the normal potassium to dismiss the result","explanation":"Normal potassium does not exclude sodium-related symptoms."},{"id":"C","text":"Assess neurological status and escalate the electrolyte change","explanation":"Symptomatic hyponatremia can be clinically important."},{"id":"D","text":"Assume the confusion is normal tiredness","explanation":"The abnormal sodium requires a clinical assessment."}],"point":"Hyponatremia can cause neurological symptoms during susceptible treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--7-day--low_na","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::low_na"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A newly drowsy patient receiving TEGRETOL — carbamazepine wants to walk alone. What is the best immediate plan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Allow walking because oxygen saturation is normal","explanation":"Normal SpO2 does not exclude impaired balance or sedation."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the previous day's mobility assessment","explanation":"A new change requires current assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Assist mobility and reassess sedation","explanation":"New drowsiness makes unassisted walking unsafe."},{"id":"D","text":"Delay help until the next scheduled observation","explanation":"The current fall risk needs action now."}],"point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--7-day--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient stops chronic TEGRETOL — carbamazepine because no seizures have occurred recently. What is the best teaching?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Restart with twice the usual dose without advice","explanation":"Unsupervised dose doubling can cause toxicity."},{"id":"B","text":"Replace missed doses with any available anticonvulsant","explanation":"Anticonvulsants are not freely interchangeable."},{"id":"C","text":"Recent seizure freedom proves treatment is unnecessary","explanation":"Seizure freedom may reflect effective treatment."},{"id":"D","text":"Seek an authorized plan before changing treatment","explanation":"Abrupt interruption can lead to loss of seizure control."}],"point":"An interruption of chronic anticonvulsant treatment needs an authorized plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--7-day--anticonvulsant_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::anticonvulsant_stop"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A supplied TEGRETOL — carbamazepine product has a different route or release type from the prescription. What is the safest action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Substitute if the tablet strength looks similar","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish release or route suitability."},{"id":"B","text":"Verify the exact product and authorized substitution","explanation":"Matching a drug name does not establish formulation equivalence."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the previous patient's preparation instructions","explanation":"Preparation must match this exact product and order."},{"id":"D","text":"Crush the product to make absorption equivalent","explanation":"Crushing can alter release and may be unsafe."}],"point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-carbamazepine--7-day--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-carbamazepine::formulation"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":6}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-carbamazepine","title":"CARBAMAZEPINE TABLET, EXTENDED RELEASE [GOLDEN STATE MEDICAL SUPPLY, INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=1922a7cd-6a43-dab5-e063-6294a90a3d8f"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Anticonvulsant; voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker; neuropathic-pain agent.","mechanism":"Stabilizes inactivated voltage-gated sodium channels, reducing sustained repetitive neuronal firing.","use":"Seizure disorders and selected neuropathic-pain conditions such as trigeminal neuralgia. In an orthopaedic patient, confirm whether it is being continued for epilepsy or prescribed for a specific neuropathic-pain indication.","administration":"PO in the ordered formulation. Do not crush or interchange extended-release and immediate-release products without checking the product. Do not abruptly stop chronic therapy unless specifically directed.","monitor":"• New rash is a major red flag because SJS/TEN can occur. • Monitor CBC when ordered; rare aplastic anemia/agranulocytosis and other cytopenias are important. • Monitor sodium because SIADH/hyponatremia can cause confusion, weakness, seizures and falls. • Watch dizziness, drowsiness and ataxia. • Carbamazepine has many drug interactions because it induces hepatic enzymes.","why":"Sodium-channel blockade reduces repetitive neuronal firing, helping seizures and selected neuropathic pain, but the drug has important hematologic, dermatologic, electrolyte and interaction risks.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED repeatedly in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex, including 27 Oct 2026. Tegretol is carbamazepine; verify the exact strength and formulation because immediate-release and prolonged-release products are not automatically interchangeable."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Anticonvulsant; voltage-gated sodium-channel blocker; neuropathic-pain agent.","id":"section-0-76e209ace2e3af74"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Stabilizes inactivated voltage-gated sodium channels, reducing sustained repetitive neuronal firing.","id":"section-1-8d16814724b85548"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Seizure disorders and selected neuropathic-pain conditions such as trigeminal neuralgia. In an orthopaedic patient, confirm whether it is being continued for epilepsy or prescribed for a specific neuropathic-pain indication.","id":"section-2-3205a531cd580e7e"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"PO in the ordered formulation. Do not crush or interchange extended-release and immediate-release products without checking the product. Do not abruptly stop chronic therapy unless specifically directed.","id":"section-3-918d441b109c4432"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• New rash is a major red flag because SJS/TEN can occur. • Monitor CBC when ordered; rare aplastic anemia/agranulocytosis and other cytopenias are important. • Monitor sodium because SIADH/hyponatremia can cause confusion, weakness, seizures and falls. • Watch dizziness, drowsiness and ataxia. • Carbamazepine has many drug interactions because it induces hepatic enzymes.","id":"section-4-32aa6f169ac105bf"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Sodium-channel blockade reduces repetitive neuronal firing, helping seizures and selected neuropathic pain, but the drug has important hematologic, dermatologic, electrolyte and interaction risks.","id":"section-5-d7704539480c8457"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED repeatedly in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex, including 27 Oct 2026. Tegretol is carbamazepine; verify the exact strength and formulation because immediate-release and prolonged-release products are not automatically interchangeable.","id":"section-6-9f4ef573b740b816"}]},{"itemId":"trolley-29","title":"METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"trolley-29--standard","itemId":"trolley-29","itemType":"medication","title":"METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits hepatic HMG-CoA reductase","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Dopamine blockade contributes to antiemesis and extrapyramidal adverse effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Mu agonism and reuptake inhibition","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Dopamine blockade contributes to antiemesis and extrapyramidal adverse effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Airway anti-inflammatory and bronchodilator effects","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Dopamine blockade contributes to antiemesis and extrapyramidal adverse effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Promotes upper-GI motility","explanation":"Dopamine blockade contributes to antiemesis and extrapyramidal adverse effects."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"D2 antagonist/prokinetic","explanation":"Dopamine D2-antagonist/prokinetic antiemetic."},{"id":"B","text":"Osmotic laxative","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Dopamine D2-antagonist/prokinetic antiemetic."},{"id":"C","text":"Proton-pump inhibitor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Dopamine D2-antagonist/prokinetic antiemetic."},{"id":"D","text":"First-generation H1 antagonist","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Dopamine D2-antagonist/prokinetic antiemetic."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::drug-class"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Treatment of nausea with a prokinetic role","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Antiemetic/prokinetic for nausea and vomiting; now also ward-observed in IBD2, where postoperative nausea/vomiting is a common practical context."},{"id":"D","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Soon after METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN, a patient develops sustained neck twisting and upward eye deviation. Which reaction best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Serotonin toxicity","explanation":"The case does not describe the broader febrile, hyperreflexic, autonomic pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Opioid respiratory toxicity","explanation":"This does not explain the characteristic sustained postures."},{"id":"C","text":"Orthostatic hypotension","explanation":"A postural BP fall does not explain this movement pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Acute dystonia","explanation":"Sustained abnormal muscle postures are characteristic of a dystonic reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Dopamine blockade can cause acute dystonia or akathisia.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"trolley-29--standard--eps","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::eps"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which gastrointestinal history most directly requires clarification before METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Mechanical bowel obstruction","explanation":"The drug’s GI action may be inappropriate or unsafe in this setting."},{"id":"B","text":"Previously controlled acid reflux","explanation":"This is a different gastrointestinal condition."},{"id":"C","text":"Previously treated oral candidiasis","explanation":"This does not identify the relevant mechanical GI concern."},{"id":"D","text":"Previous uncomplicated dietary constipation","explanation":"This does not establish a mechanical obstruction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Stimulating GI motility may be unsafe in mechanical obstruction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"trolley-29--standard--gi_obstruction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::gi_obstruction"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN injection order is valid, but the minimum administration time is unclear. Which source should resolve preparation and rate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The IV rate previously used for a different drug class","explanation":"Another drug’s administration rate is not transferable."},{"id":"B","text":"The exact product instructions and authorized local protocol","explanation":"Concentration, formulation, and indication can change administration requirements."},{"id":"C","text":"The instructions for a similar-looking vial on the trolley","explanation":"Appearance does not establish an equivalent product or rate."},{"id":"D","text":"The instructions for an oral product of the same name","explanation":"Oral instructions do not establish an IV administration rate."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"IV administration speed follows the exact product and indication.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--standard--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::slow_iv"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"trolley-29--advanced","itemId":"trolley-29","itemType":"medication","title":"METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"After METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN, nausea improves but the patient develops severe inner restlessness and repetitive pacing. Which adverse effect best explains the new behavior?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"This does not account for the new movement behavior."},{"id":"B","text":"Opioid intoxication","explanation":"This is more associated with central depression than marked motor restlessness."},{"id":"C","text":"Acute dystonia","explanation":"Dystonia causes sustained abnormal postures rather than this predominant restlessness."},{"id":"D","text":"Akathisia","explanation":"Motor restlessness after dopamine blockade fits this extrapyramidal reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Dopamine blockade can cause acute dystonia or akathisia.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"trolley-29--advanced--eps","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::eps"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient prescribed METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN has worsening pain and distension with documented mechanical obstruction. Which reasoning best supports reviewing the order?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The presence of obstruction establishes a need for stronger prokinetic action","explanation":"Stimulation is not an appropriate assumption in a mechanically obstructed bowel."},{"id":"B","text":"The presence of pain establishes functional spasm as the cause","explanation":"Mechanical obstruction is already documented."},{"id":"C","text":"The underlying obstruction changes the safety of symptomatic treatment","explanation":"The mechanism and cause matter beyond relief of symptoms."},{"id":"D","text":"The presence of distension establishes inadequate acid suppression","explanation":"Distension does not establish an acid-related mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Stimulating GI motility may be unsafe in mechanical obstruction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"trolley-29--advanced--gi_obstruction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::gi_obstruction"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient with Parkinson disease is prescribed metoclopramide. Which pharmacological action explains the need for review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dopamine D2-receptor blockade","explanation":"This can worsen parkinsonian motor symptoms."},{"id":"B","text":"Angiotensin-II receptor blockade","explanation":"This is unrelated to its extrapyramidal effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Beta2-receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a bronchodilator mechanism, not metoclopramide’s concern."},{"id":"D","text":"Gastric proton-pump inhibition","explanation":"Acid suppression does not explain dopamine-related motor worsening."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Dopamine blockade can worsen parkinsonian symptoms.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--advanced--parkinsonism","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::parkinsonism"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"numeric-entry","stem":"The observed metoclopramide ampoule contains 10 mg in 2 mL. Calculate its concentration in mg/mL.","answer":5,"unit":"mg/mL","tolerance":0,"explanation":"10 mg divided by 2 mL = 5 mg/mL.","point":"Convert the stated 10 mg/2 mL ampoule to mg/mL.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--advanced--metoclopramide_concentration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::metoclopramide_concentration"]}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"trolley-29--7-day","itemId":"trolley-29","itemType":"medication","title":"METOCLOPRAMIDE — PRAMIN","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After metoclopramide, a patient's neck twists painfully and the eyes deviate upward. Which adverse reaction is most likely?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated antimuscarinic dry mouth","explanation":"Dry mouth does not explain the movement disorder."},{"id":"B","text":"Acute dystonia","explanation":"Sustained abnormal contractions can follow dopamine blockade."},{"id":"C","text":"Simple postural dizziness","explanation":"Dizziness does not explain sustained abnormal postures."},{"id":"D","text":"Expected relief of gastric stasis","explanation":"Improved motility does not produce this motor pattern."}],"point":"Dopamine blockade can cause acute dystonia or akathisia.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"trolley-29--7-day--eps","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::eps"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A metoclopramide order is written for a patient with confirmed mechanical bowel obstruction. Why clarify it?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Obstruction proves that every antiemetic is appropriate","explanation":"Antiemetic suitability depends on its specific action and contraindications."},{"id":"B","text":"The medicine suppresses all intestinal movement","explanation":"Metoclopramide has prokinetic actions."},{"id":"C","text":"Increasing motility can be unsafe against an obstruction","explanation":"Mechanical obstruction is a safety concern for prokinetic therapy."},{"id":"D","text":"The drug is primarily a replacement electrolyte","explanation":"It is not an electrolyte replacement."}],"point":"Stimulating GI motility may be unsafe in mechanical obstruction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"trolley-29--7-day--gi_obstruction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::gi_obstruction"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which history most directly raises concern before metoclopramide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Parkinson disease with troublesome motor symptoms","explanation":"Dopamine blockade can worsen parkinsonism."},{"id":"B","text":"A healed fracture without neurological disease","explanation":"It does not establish the same contraindication concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Seasonal rhinitis without medication","explanation":"This does not explain dopamine-related motor risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Constipation treated with dietary advice","explanation":"This is not the specific dopamine-related concern."}],"point":"Dopamine blockade can worsen parkinsonian symptoms.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--7-day--parkinsonism","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::parkinsonism"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Two injection products with the same drug name have different preparation instructions. Which should guide administration?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The rate remembered from another manufacturer's product","explanation":"Similar names do not guarantee identical preparation."},{"id":"B","text":"The fastest rate used elsewhere on the ward","explanation":"Ward custom cannot replace verified instructions."},{"id":"C","text":"The remaining time in the medication round","explanation":"Convenience does not determine a safe administration rate."},{"id":"D","text":"The supplied product's instructions and authorized order","explanation":"Route, dilution and rate are product- and indication-specific."}],"point":"IV administration speed follows the exact product and indication.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"trolley-29--7-day--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["trolley-29::slow_iv"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":4}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-trolley-29","title":"METOCLOPRAMIDE (METOCLOPRAMIDE HYDROCHLORIDE) INJECTION [HENRY SCHEIN, INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=f50c8fb4-23c4-4762-95bb-e4ca7b289a4d"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Dopamine D2-antagonist/prokinetic antiemetic.","mechanism":"Blocks dopamine D2 receptors and promotes upper-GI motility, reducing nausea and vomiting.","use":"Antiemetic/prokinetic for nausea and vomiting; now also ward-observed in IBD2, where postoperative nausea/vomiting is a common practical context.","administration":"The local METO-ALISON ampoule contains 10 mg/2 mL = 5 mg/mL for IV or IM use. For ordinary small IV antiemetic doses, administration should be slow rather than a rapid push; use the actual ampoule insert for the exact minimum time.","monitor":"• Watch for acute dystonia, akathisia/restlessness and other extrapyramidal reactions, especially after parenteral dosing. • Stop/escalate for sustained neck/jaw/eye deviation, severe restlessness, rigidity or other acute abnormal movements. • Avoid routine use in known mechanical GI obstruction/perforation or where stimulating motility is unsafe. • Parkinsonian symptoms can worsen because of dopamine blockade. • Rapid IV administration can make restlessness/anxiety more prominent.","why":"D2 blockade helps nausea and gastric emptying, but the same dopamine blockade can produce acute movement disorders.","availability":"YES. The Palestinian spelling is PRAMIN. A locally registered product, METO-ALISON 10 mg/2 mL, is IV/IM; Palestinian data also list metoclopramide 10-mg ampoules and tablets."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Dopamine D2-antagonist/prokinetic antiemetic.","id":"section-0-b20c166e7246ce79"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Blocks dopamine D2 receptors and promotes upper-GI motility, reducing nausea and vomiting.","id":"section-1-87a070623629f746"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Antiemetic/prokinetic for nausea and vomiting; now also ward-observed in IBD2, where postoperative nausea/vomiting is a common practical context.","id":"section-2-5fe5afe3027c6122"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"The local METO-ALISON ampoule contains 10 mg/2 mL = 5 mg/mL for IV or IM use. For ordinary small IV antiemetic doses, administration should be slow rather than a rapid push; use the actual ampoule insert for the exact minimum time.","id":"section-3-a30fa3ec44dddd16"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Watch for acute dystonia, akathisia/restlessness and other extrapyramidal reactions, especially after parenteral dosing. • Stop/escalate for sustained neck/jaw/eye deviation, severe restlessness, rigidity or other acute abnormal movements. • Avoid routine use in known mechanical GI obstruction/perforation or where stimulating motility is unsafe. • Parkinsonian symptoms can worsen because of dopamine blockade. • Rapid IV administration can make restlessness/anxiety more prominent.","id":"section-4-19ef6d8076dede9e"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"D2 blockade helps nausea and gastric emptying, but the same dopamine blockade can produce acute movement disorders.","id":"section-5-65fa7dfe983df65e"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"YES. The Palestinian spelling is PRAMIN. A locally registered product, METO-ALISON 10 mg/2 mL, is IV/IM; Palestinian data also list metoclopramide 10-mg ampoules and tablets.","id":"section-6-21e6c1fa9a1fceef"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin","title":"EPANUTIN — phenytoin","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--standard","itemId":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin","itemType":"medication","title":"EPANUTIN — phenytoin","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of EPANUTIN — phenytoin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits cyclooxygenase and reduces prostaglandins","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Carbamazepine and phenytoin limit repetitive neuronal firing through sodium-channel effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Stabilizes inactivated voltage-gated sodium channels","explanation":"Carbamazepine and phenytoin limit repetitive neuronal firing through sodium-channel effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Blocks mineralocorticoid receptors","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Carbamazepine and phenytoin limit repetitive neuronal firing through 