tion/treatment of selected seizure disorders. In trauma/orthopaedic patients it may also appear when a patient has epilepsy or seizure risk from associated head injury; confirm the actual indication."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Nystagmus, ataxia, and slurred speech develop during EPANUTIN — phenytoin therapy. Which explanation best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Phenytoin neurotoxicity","explanation":"The cluster is characteristic of excessive neurological drug effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Opioid-related constipation","explanation":"This does not explain the ocular and coordination findings."},{"id":"C","text":"Serotonin toxicity","explanation":"The case lacks the characteristic hyperreflexic, febrile autonomic pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Vancomycin infusion intolerance","explanation":"This is a different treatment and presentation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize phenytoin neurological toxicity and interpret concentrations in context.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--standard--phenytoin_toxicity","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::phenytoin_toxicity"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which new finding during EPANUTIN — phenytoin therapy most strongly suggests a serious cutaneous drug reaction?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dry mouth without a rash","explanation":"This points toward a different adverse-effect pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Mild tremor without a rash","explanation":"This does not establish the skin and mucosal syndrome."},{"id":"C","text":"Rash with mucosal involvement","explanation":"This raises concern for severe hypersensitivity, including SJS/TEN."},{"id":"D","text":"Constipation without a rash","explanation":"This does not identify a severe cutaneous reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A new severe rash can indicate a serious anticonvulsant reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--standard--rash_serious","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::rash_serious"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin","itemType":"medication","title":"EPANUTIN — phenytoin","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient taking EPANUTIN — phenytoin has low albumin, new ataxia, and a total concentration within the printed reference range. Which issue most limits reassurance from that result?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Ataxia is unrelated to neurological drug exposure","explanation":"It is a recognized toxicity finding."},{"id":"B","text":"Altered protein binding can change active exposure","explanation":"Total concentration must be interpreted with albumin and clinical findings."},{"id":"C","text":"A total value measures renal clearance directly","explanation":"It measures concentration, not a direct renal-clearance test."},{"id":"D","text":"A normal total value establishes unchanged protein binding","explanation":"The low albumin directly challenges that assumption."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize phenytoin neurological toxicity and interpret concentrations in context.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--advanced--phenytoin_toxicity","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::phenytoin_toxicity"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient taking EPANUTIN — phenytoin develops a new rash and painful mouth lesions while seizures remain controlled. Which conclusion best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Drug withdrawal explains the mucosal lesions","explanation":"No interruption is described, and the pattern raises a different concern."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced absorption explains the mucosal lesions","explanation":"Reduced absorption does not explain this cutaneous reaction."},{"id":"C","text":"Loss of seizure control explains the mucosal lesions","explanation":"The seizures remain controlled, and this does not explain the rash."},{"id":"D","text":"Therapeutic benefit and serious hypersensitivity may coexist","explanation":"Seizure control does not exclude a dangerous drug reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A new severe rash can indicate a serious anticonvulsant reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--advanced--rash_serious","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::rash_serious"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient takes chronic EPANUTIN — phenytoin for epilepsy and has omitted several doses because seizures stopped. Which concern is most relevant?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A reduction in the medicine’s enzyme-inducing potential alone","explanation":"That does not address the immediate loss of established seizure treatment."},{"id":"B","text":"An immediate increase in renal drug clearance","explanation":"Dose omission does not itself establish a renal-clearance change."},{"id":"C","text":"Loss of maintained seizure protection","explanation":"Control while treated does not establish that interruption is safe."},{"id":"D","text":"An expected increase in anticonvulsant exposure","explanation":"Missed doses generally reduce exposure rather than increase it."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"An interruption of chronic anticonvulsant treatment needs an authorized plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--advanced--anticonvulsant_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::anticonvulsant_stop"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"The prescribed EPANUTIN — phenytoin and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."},{"id":"B","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."},{"id":"C","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."},{"id":"D","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--advanced--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::formulation"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A concentration is near the upper laboratory reference limit during EPANUTIN — phenytoin therapy. Which information is necessary before interpreting it as clinically acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The previous normal level without the recent dose history","explanation":"Earlier exposure does not establish current safety."},{"id":"B","text":"Dose/sample timing, organ function, and toxicity findings","explanation":"The result requires both sampling context and clinical interpretation."},{"id":"C","text":"The reference interval and prescribed dose without sample timing","explanation":"A mistimed result can be misleading despite a familiar dose."},{"id":"D","text":"The current symptom score without the level’s sampling context","explanation":"Clinical findings matter but cannot correct an unknown sampling time."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Interpret therapeutic drug concentrations with timing and clinical context.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--advanced--drug_levels","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::drug_levels"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient taking phenytoin has low albumin, ataxia, and a total level reported within range. Which interpretation is most defensible?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Ataxia proves insufficient anticonvulsant exposure","explanation":"Ataxia is a toxicity signal, not proof of underdosing."},{"id":"B","text":"An in-range total value excludes toxicity despite the symptoms","explanation":"Total concentration alone can be misleading when binding changes."},{"id":"C","text":"Binding changes and clinical toxicity can make the total value misleading","explanation":"Interpret levels with albumin, organ function, and symptoms."},{"id":"D","text":"Low albumin lowers total levels and therefore excludes toxicity","explanation":"Less protein binding can increase the unbound fraction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Protein binding and organ function affect interpretation of phenytoin levels.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--advanced--phenytoin_albumin","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::phenytoin_albumin"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin","itemType":"medication","title":"EPANUTIN — phenytoin","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1