ensitivity."}],"point":"A new severe rash can indicate a serious anticonvulsant reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--7-day--rash_serious","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::rash_serious"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient stops chronic EPANUTIN — phenytoin because no seizures have occurred recently. What is the best teaching?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replace missed doses with any available anticonvulsant","explanation":"Anticonvulsants are not freely interchangeable."},{"id":"B","text":"Recent seizure freedom proves treatment is unnecessary","explanation":"Seizure freedom may reflect effective treatment."},{"id":"C","text":"Restart with twice the usual dose without advice","explanation":"Unsupervised dose doubling can cause toxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"Seek an authorized plan before changing treatment","explanation":"Abrupt interruption can lead to loss of seizure control."}],"point":"An interruption of chronic anticonvulsant treatment needs an authorized plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin--7-day--anticonvulsant_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-epanutin-phenytoin::anticonvulsant_stop"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A supplied EPANUTIN — phenytoin product has a different route or release type from the prescription. What is the safest action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the previous patient's preparation instructions","explanation":"Preparation must match this exact product and order."},{"id":"B","text":"Verify the exact product and authorized substitution","explanation":"Matching a drug name does not establish formulation equivalence."},{"id":"C","text":"Crush the product to make absorption equivalent","explanation":"Crushing can alter release and may be unsafe."},{"id":"D","text":"Substitute if the tablet strength looks similar","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish release or route suitability."}]