t renal result","explanation":"Colour does not reliably assess filtration."},{"id":"D","text":"Rising creatinine with falling urine output","explanation":"Together these suggest deteriorating kidney function."}],"point":"Monitor renal function when kidney injury or altered treatment safety is relevant.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-vancomycin--7-day--renal_monitor","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-vancomycin::renal_monitor"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A vancomycin infusion was accidentally accelerated and flushing develops. What is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Finish quickly because the total dose is unchanged","explanation":"Rate can matter even when the total dose is correct."},{"id":"B","text":"Give the next infusion faster to shorten exposure","explanation":"Faster infusion can increase infusion-reaction risk."},{"id":"C","text":"Pause the infusion and assess airway, BP and symptoms","explanation":"An infusion reaction needs assessment; severe symptoms also require emergency review."},{"id":"D","text":"Record flushing without checking circulation","explanation":"Hypotension and systemic involvement must be assessed."}],"point":"Rapid systemic vancomycin infusion can produce an infusion reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-vancomycin--7-day--vanco_infusion","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-vancomycin::vanco_infusion"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A drug concentration arrives without a recorded sampling time. What should the nurse clarify?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Sampling time, recent doses and current symptoms","explanation":"These are needed to interpret measured exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"The printed laboratory range without sampling time","explanation":"A range alone cannot establish correct interpretation."},{"id":"C","text":"The medicine's prescribed daily total alone","explanation":"Dose alone does not establish concentration timing or toxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"The last normal concentration from weeks earlier","explanation":"Exposure may have changed since that result."}],"point":"Interpret therapeutic drug concentrations with timing and clinical context.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-vancomycin--7-day--drug_levels","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-vancomycin::drug_levels"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After several doses of VANCOMYCIN, fever persists and the patient deteriorates. What should guide the next review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An old culture from a different infection","explanation":"It may not represent the current pathogen or susceptibility."},{"id":"B","text":"Clinical course, cultures and the authorized treatment plan","explanation":"These help assess diagnosis, susceptibility and response."},{"id":"C","text":"The white cell count in isolation","explanation":"A single laboratory value does not replace the full clinical review."},{"id":"D","text":"The fever response to an antipyretic alone","explanation":"Temporary fever relief does not prove antimicrobial effectiveness."}],"point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-vancomycin--7-day--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-vancomycin::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":4}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-vancomycin","title":"VANCOMYCIN (VANCOMYCIN HYDROCHLORIDE) INJECTION, POWDER, LYOPHILIZED, FOR SOLUTION [SAGENT PHARMACEUTICALS]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=06dcfd36-942f-4752-86ad-4c5a6fc3e48f"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Glycopeptide antibacterial.","mechanism":"Binds D-Ala-D-Ala termini of peptidoglycan precursors and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","use":"Serious susceptible gram-positive infections, including MRSA when appropriate. In orthopaedics it may be used for severe wound, bone/joint or hardware-associated infection depending on cultures, local resistance patterns and the treatment plan.","administration":"IV for systemic infection. Use the exact product-specific dilution/infusion instructions and local protocol. Rapid infusion can cause a vancomycin infusion reaction and hypotension, so do not give as an unmonitored rapid IV push.","monitor":"• Monitor renal function and urine output. • Therapeutic drug monitoring may be required for systemic therapy; follow the hospital/pharmacy protocol. • Watch for flushing, pruritus, erythema and hypotension during infusion; slow/stop and escalate according to protocol if a significant infusion reaction occurs. • Monitor for nephrotoxicity, especially with other nephrotoxic drugs. • Review cultures and clinical response.","why":"Vancomycin inhibits gram-positive bacterial cell-wall synthesis. Exposure that is too low can fail therapy, while excessive exposure and nephrotoxic combinations increase kidney-injury risk.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Exact vial/ready-to-use formulation and concentration were not recorded; verify the product, concentration and order."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Glycopeptide antibacterial.","id":"section-0-555f8b3bf4d48232"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Binds D-Ala-D-Ala termini of peptidoglycan precursors and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","id":"section-1-cee32b1638eae996"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Serious susceptible gram-positive infections, including MRSA when appropriate. In orthopaedics it may be used for severe wound, bone/joint or hardware-associated infection depending on cultures, local resistance patterns and the treatment plan.","id":"section-2-fc3e7decb4bc771e"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"IV for systemic infection. Use the exact product-specific dilution/infusion instructions and local protocol. Rapid infusion can cause a vancomycin infusion reaction and hypotension, so do not give as an unmonitored rapid IV push.","id":"section-3-eb9857dcd10181f3"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Monitor renal function and urine output. • Therapeutic drug monitoring may be required for systemic therapy; follow the hospital/pharmacy protocol. • Watch for flushing, pruritus, erythema and hypotension during infusion; slow/stop and escalate according to protocol if a significant infusion reaction occurs. • Monitor for nephrotoxicity, especially with other nephrotoxic drugs. • Review cultures and clinical response.","id":"section-4-93ec863c7b638d0d"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Vancomycin inhibits gram-positive bacterial cell-wall synthesis. Exposure that is too low can fail therapy, while excessive exposure and nephrotoxic combinations increase kidney-injury risk.","id":"section-5-5dffb87f8ff16a9f"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Exact vial/ready-to-use formulation and concentration were not recorded; verify the product, concentration and order.","id":"section-6-2023fa9853d4fa67"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin","title":"LEVOX — levofloxacin","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--standard","itemId":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin","itemType":"medication","title":"LEVOX — levofloxacin","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all