xplanation":"That is not the risk established by these ECG and electrolyte findings."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"QT-prolonging treatment requires attention to rhythm and electrolyte context.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--advanced--qt","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::qt"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"An insulin-treated patient starts levofloxacin and develops sweating and confusion. Which interpretation best guides assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Assume hyperglycemia because antibiotics raise glucose consistently","explanation":"Both low and high glucose are recognized concerns."},{"id":"B","text":"Use renal function alone to distinguish low from high glucose","explanation":"Renal function informs risk but does not replace glucose measurement."},{"id":"C","text":"Assume hypoglycemia without measuring because QT risk proves it","explanation":"QT risk does not establish the blood glucose."},{"id":"D","text":"Check glucose; fluoroquinolones can disturb glucose in either direction","explanation":"Symptoms require a measurement rather than an assumed direction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Fluoroquinolones can disturb glucose control in susceptible patients.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--advanced--dysglycemia","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::dysglycemia"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient develops persistent watery diarrhea after LEVOX — levofloxacin. Which explanation should be considered during assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Antibiotic-associated disturbance of intestinal flora","explanation":"This can permit C. difficile-associated disease."},{"id":"B","text":"Therapeutic stimulation of intestinal glucose absorption","explanation":"This does not explain the antibiotic-associated diarrhea warning."},{"id":"C","text":"An immediate IgE-mediated airway reaction","explanation":"The case describes a gastrointestinal pattern rather than acute anaphylaxis."},{"id":"D","text":"An intended increase in osmotic laxative activity","explanation":"The antibiotic is not being used as an osmotic laxative."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--advanced--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin","itemType":"medication","title":"LEVOX — levofloxacin","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Oral levofloxacin and an iron supplement are scheduled together. What should the nurse check?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Whether both can be crushed into the same mixture","explanation":"Mixing does not prevent the absorption interaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Whether extra iron improves antibiotic absorption","explanation":"Iron can bind the antibiotic instead."},{"id":"C","text":"Whether IV compatibility guarantees oral compatibility","explanation":"IV compatibility does not address gastrointestinal chelation."},{"id":"D","text":"Product-directed separation to reduce chelation","explanation":"Multivalent cations can reduce oral fluoroquinolone absorption."}],"point":"Multivalent cations can reduce oral fluoroquinolone absorption.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day--oral_cations","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::oral_cations"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving LEVOX — levofloxacin develops acute kidney injury. Which prescription detail most needs review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose against symptom relief alone","explanation":"Benefit does not exclude accumulation or a need for adjustment."},{"id":"B","text":"Dose and interval against current renal function","explanation":"Reduced clearance can require regimen adjustment."},{"id":"C","text":"Dose against the last normal creatinine only","explanation":"An old normal result does not establish current clearance."},{"id":"D","text":"Interval against serum potassium alone","explanation":"Potassium does not quantify the renal clearance needed for this review."}],"point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking levofloxacin reports new Achilles pain. What advice is appropriate while obtaining prompt review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Double the next dose to treat presumed infection there","explanation":"Unprescribed dose changes are unsafe and miss the adverse effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Ignore the pain if there is no visible bruising","explanation":"Tendon injury can present without bruising."},{"id":"C","text":"Avoid stressing the tendon and report the symptom","explanation":"Fluoroquinolone-associated tendon injury needs assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Exercise through the pain to restore strength","explanation":"Loading may worsen a developing tendon injury."}],"point":"Fluoroquinolone-associated new tendon pain needs prompt review.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day--tendon","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::tendon"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During LEVOX — levofloxacin treatment, which symptom needs review for peripheral nerve injury?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An old healed scar without sensory change","explanation":"A stable scar is not new treatment-related sensory loss."},{"id":"B","text":"Unchanged sensation and normal movement","explanation":"This does not demonstrate new neuropathy."},{"id":"C","text":"A brief taste change after swallowing","explanation":"Taste change does not establish peripheral nerve injury."},{"id":"D","text":"New persistent burning and numbness in the feet","explanation":"These are compatible with peripheral neuropathy."}],"point":"New sensory symptoms during susceptible treatment need review.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day--neuropathy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::neuropathy"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving LEVOX — levofloxacin has QT prolongation with low K and Mg. What is the main safety concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increased risk of ventricular dysrhythmia","explanation":"QT prolongation plus electrolyte depletion raises proarrhythmic concern."},{"id":"B","text":"A reason to discontinue rhythm observation","explanation":"The abnormality calls for closer review and monitoring."},{"id":"C","text":"Proof that the medicine has no cardiac effect","explanation":"QT prolongation is a cardiac safety signal."},{"id":"D","text":"A normal expected finding requiring no correction review","explanation":"Electrolyte abnormalities and QT change need assessment."}],"point":"QT-prolonging treatment requires attention to rhythm and electrolyte context.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day--qt","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::qt"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After antibiotic treatment, a patient reports frequent watery stools and abdominal cramps. What is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Ignore it unless the next antibiotic dose is due","explanation":"Assessment is driven by symptoms, not the dosing time."},{"id":"B","text":"Assume the infection is resolving through the bowel","explanation":"Diarrhea does not establish treatment success."},{"id":"C","text":"Assess and report possible antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"Significant diarrhea requires evaluation, including C. difficile when indicated."},{"id":"D","text":"Recommend an antidiarrheal without further assessment","explanation":"Symptom suppression may be inappropriate before evaluating colitis."}],"point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After several doses of LEVOX — levofloxacin, fever persists and the patient deteriorates. What should guide the next review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An old culture from a different infection","explanation":"It may not represent the current pathogen or susceptibility."},{"id":"B","text":"Clinical course, cultures and the authorized treatment plan","explanation":"These help assess diagnosis, susceptibility and response."},{"id":"C","text":"The fever response to an antipyretic alone","explanation":"Temporary fever relief does not prove antimicrobial effectiveness."},{"id":"D","text":"The white cell count in isolation","explanation":"A single laboratory value does not replace the full clinical review."}],"point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-levox-levofloxacin--7-day--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-levox-levofloxacin::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":7}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-levox-levofloxacin","title":"LEVOFLOXACIN TABLET, FILM COATED [PD-RX PHARMACEUTICALS, INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b8defac4-9deb-433b-928e-0ef4ee0123a2"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Fluoroquinolone antibacterial.","mechanism":"Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV, blocking DNA replication.","use":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infections. Palestinian product information includes respiratory, urinary/kidney, and skin/soft-tissue infections; use should match the actual infection, culture data and local antimicrobial plan.","administration":"For the observed LEVOX tablet, give PO exactly as ordered. Separate oral levofloxacin from products containing multivalent cations such as iron, zinc, magnesium/aluminum antacids, or sucralfate according to the product instructions because absorption can be reduced. Renal impairment may require dose/interval adjustment.","monitor":"• Important fluoroquinolone warnings include tendinitis/tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy and CNS effects. • Stop/escalate new tendon pain or swelling rather than encouraging exercise through it. • Review renal function. • Watch for QT-prolongation risk, dysglycemia and significant diarrhea/C. difficile where relevant. • Use only for a susceptible bacterial indication; avoid unnecessary broad-spectrum exposure.","why":"Levofloxacin inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV. Its broad activity is useful in selected infections, but potentially disabling adverse effects make indication and monitoring important.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Palestinian MoH records LEVOX 500 mg and 750 mg film-coated tablets containing levofloxacin; verify the exact strength in hand."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Fluoroquinolone antibacterial.","id":"section-0-b14861eab9097aed"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV, blocking DNA replication.","id":"section-1-925f25e9d618d5d4"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infections. Palestinian product information includes respiratory, urinary/kidney, and skin/soft-tissue infections; use should match the actual infection, culture data and local antimicrobial plan.","id":"section-2-9bc679b584458c73"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"For the observed LEVOX tablet, give PO exactly as ordered. Separate oral levofloxacin from products containing multivalent cations such as iron, zinc, magnesium/aluminum antacids, or sucralfate according to the product instructions because absorption can be reduced. Renal impairment may require dose/interval adjustment.","id":"section-3-8cfd3d6148b41a14"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Important fluoroquinolone warnings include tendinitis/tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy and CNS effects. • Stop/escalate new tendon pain or swelling rather than encouraging exercise through it. • Review renal function. • Watch for QT-prolongation risk, dysglycemia and significant diarrhea/C. difficile where relevant. • Use only for a susceptible bacterial indication; avoid unnecessary broad-spectrum exposure.","id":"section-4-f7ef9e0c992e73e7"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Levofloxacin inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV. Its broad activity is useful in selected infections, but potentially disabling adverse effects make indication and monitoring important.","id":"section-5-1c007c94a41208c1"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Palestinian MoH records LEVOX 500 mg and 750 mg film-coated tablets containing levofloxacin; verify the exact strength in hand.","id":"section-6-960708ae601d1c6d"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac","title":"KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard","itemId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac","itemType":"medication","title":"KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Forms metabolites that damage microbial DNA","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Slows AV-nodal calcium-dependent conduction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Promotes upper-GI motility","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibits cyclooxygenase and reduces prostaglandins","explanation":"Reduced prostaglandins explain analgesia, anti-inflammatory effects, and important adverse effects."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"NSAID","explanation":"NSAID analgesic."},{"id":"B","text":"Non-opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: NSAID analgesic."},{"id":"C","text":"Opioid/monoamine analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: NSAID analgesic."},{"id":"D","text":"Opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: NSAID analgesic."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::drug-class"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Short-term treatment of acute pain","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Short-term treatment of moderately severe acute pain, often postoperative or trauma-related. Current ketorolac labeling limits total therapy duration to no more than 5 days in adults."},{"id":"B","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Replacement therapy for an adrenal crisis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which new finding in a patient taking KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine most strongly suggests an upper-GI complication?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pale, greasy stool","explanation":"This points toward a different gastrointestinal process."},{"id":"B","text":"Dry, hard stool","explanation":"This suggests constipation rather than characteristic upper-GI blood loss."},{"id":"C","text":"Small, formed stool","explanation":"This does not identify the bleeding pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Black, tarry stool","explanation":"Melena can indicate upper gastrointestinal bleeding."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_gi"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which situation makes the renal safety of KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine particularly important to reassess?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Improved mobility with controlled pain","explanation":"This reflects recovery rather than increased renal risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Improved appetite with stable weight","explanation":"This does not identify a new renal-perfusion concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Vomiting with volume depletion","explanation":"Reduced effective circulating volume increases vulnerability to NSAID renal effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Stable intake with unchanged creatinine","explanation":"This does not suggest the same new renal vulnerability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_renal"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which previous reaction most directly changes the hypersensitivity assessment before KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Nausea after an opioid","explanation":"This is a different drug effect and does not identify NSAID hypersensitivity."},{"id":"B","text":"Bronchospasm after aspirin","explanation":"This may indicate a significant reaction relevant to another NSAID."},{"id":"C","text":"Flushing during vancomycin infusion","explanation":"This is a different drug-associated reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Dry cough during an ACE inhibitor","explanation":"This is a different treatment-related effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_allergy"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"For adult ketorolac, what is the labeled maximum combined duration of parenteral and oral treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Fourteen days","explanation":"This is not the ketorolac limit."},{"id":"B","text":"Five days","explanation":"The combined treatment course should not exceed five days."},{"id":"C","text":"Twenty-one days","explanation":"This substantially exceeds the short-course limit."},{"id":"D","text":"Ten days","explanation":"This exceeds the labeled combined-duration limit."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Ketorolac total treatment duration is limited.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--ketorolac_duration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::ketorolac_duration"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which paired reassessment directly measures the intended analgesic response to KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"BP and pulse during a postural change","explanation":"These assess hemodynamic tolerance rather than the pain response."},{"id":"B","text":"Alertness and respiratory effort during rest","explanation":"These assess important adverse effects rather than analgesic benefit itself."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain intensity and ability to perform the agreed activity","explanation":"These compare pain and function with baseline."},{"id":"D","text":"Stool frequency and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These assess bowel effects rather than analgesic benefit."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::pain_response"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":8},{"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac","itemType":"medication","title":"KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient develops melena during KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine treatment. Which drug action best explains the gastrointestinal vulnerability?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Blocked platelet P2Y12 receptor activation","explanation":"This describes a different antiplatelet mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased gastric proton-pump inhibition","explanation":"Acid suppression does not explain NSAID mucosal injury."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced protective gastric prostaglandin activity","explanation":"COX inhibition can impair mucosal protection."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced vitamin-K-dependent clotting-factor synthesis","explanation":"This is the principal anticoagulant mechanism of warfarin."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--advanced--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_gi"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A dehydrated patient taking KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine has rising creatinine. Which mechanism best explains the concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increased renal glucose and sodium excretion","explanation":"This is the mechanism of SGLT2 inhibitors rather than NSAIDs."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced prostaglandin support of renal perfusion","explanation":"NSAIDs can impair compensatory renal blood flow."},{"id":"C","text":"Direct mineralocorticoid-receptor blockade","explanation":"This is the principal action of spironolactone."},{"id":"D","text":"Enhanced antithrombin inhibition of factor Xa","explanation":"This is an anticoagulant mechanism, not the renal NSAID effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--advanced--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_renal"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient developed wheeze after two different NSAIDs and is now prescribed KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine. Which feature of the history matters most?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A recurrent respiratory reaction within a related drug group","explanation":"It warrants review of potential NSAID-associated hypersensitivity."},{"id":"B","text":"A reaction restricted to a local skin application","explanation":"That does not describe the systemic respiratory pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"An isolated gastrointestinal intolerance to one preparation","explanation":"That is not the history described."},{"id":"D","text":"A reaction restricted to an IV infusion rate","explanation":"The history describes reactions to related drugs, not just rate-related intolerance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--advanced--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_allergy"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient is prescribed KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine and reports taking an additional NSAID bought outside the hospital. Which issue needs clarification?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A reduction in total COX-inhibitor exposure","explanation":"Adding another NSAID does not reduce the shared exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"A need to add an antiplatelet indication","explanation":"Taking an NSAID does not establish such an indication."},{"id":"C","text":"A need to interpret the combination as opioid tolerance","explanation":"Neither the report nor the drug group establishes opioid tolerance."},{"id":"D","text":"Overlapping NSAID exposure and adverse-effect risk","explanation":"Two NSAIDs can duplicate GI and renal risk without being an intended combination."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Avoid unintended duplication of NSAIDs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--advanced--duplicate_nsaid","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::duplicate_nsaid"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac","itemType":"medication","title":"KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine treatment a patient passes black tarry stool. Which action is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Document the stool as an expected analgesic effect","explanation":"Melena is an adverse warning, not the intended effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Assess bleeding and circulation and escalate promptly","explanation":"Melena can indicate gastrointestinal bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"Offer food with the next dose and observe","explanation":"Food does not resolve suspected bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"Check the pain score before deciding to report","explanation":"Pain relief does not make possible bleeding safe."}],"point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_gi"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An NSAID-treated patient has vomiting and poor intake. Which complication becomes more likely?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increased kidney clearance from dehydration","explanation":"Dehydration does not improve renal clearance."},{"id":"B","text":"Improved filtration from prostaglandin inhibition","explanation":"Prostaglandin inhibition can compromise renal perfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Acute kidney injury from reduced renal perfusion","explanation":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-related renal risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Protection against kidney injury from less urine","explanation":"Oliguria can indicate deterioration, not protection."}],"point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_renal"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Before KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine, a patient recalls wheezing and facial swelling after aspirin. What should the nurse do?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use a smaller dose without clarifying the reaction","explanation":"A reduced dose does not establish hypersensitivity safety."},{"id":"B","text":"Separate it from other medicines by two hours","explanation":"Timing separation does not prevent NSAID hypersensitivity."},{"id":"C","text":"Give it with food and monitor dyspepsia","explanation":"Food does not prevent an allergic or bronchospastic reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Clarify the reaction before administering the NSAID","explanation":"Wheeze and facial swelling may signal serious hypersensitivity."}],"point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_allergy"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine also uses an over-the-counter anti-inflammatory. What should be checked?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Identify overlapping NSAID ingredients and exposure","explanation":"Duplication can increase gastrointestinal and renal harm."},{"id":"B","text":"Use both if the first does not fully relieve pain","explanation":"Unplanned duplication needs prescription review rather than automatic addition."},{"id":"C","text":"Separate the two NSAIDs by a meal","explanation":"Meal separation does not remove duplicate pharmacological exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Count only prescribed anti-inflammatory doses","explanation":"Nonprescription doses also contribute exposure."}],"point":"Avoid unintended duplication of NSAIDs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--duplicate_nsaid","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::duplicate_nsaid"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which note best documents benefit after prescribed KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine for pain?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The scheduled dose was administered at 10:00","explanation":"Administration is not evidence of benefit."},{"id":"B","text":"Pain fell from 7 to 3 and movement became easier","explanation":"This records symptom and functional response."},{"id":"C","text":"The medication chart contains a signed entry","explanation":"A signature confirms administration, not effectiveness."},{"id":"D","text":"The patient swallowed the medicine without difficulty","explanation":"Swallowing does not establish analgesic response."}],"point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::pain_response"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":5}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac","title":"KETOROLAC TROMETHAMINE INJECTION, SOLUTION KETROLAC TROMETHAMINE (KETOROLAC TROMETHAMINE) INJECTION, SOLUTION [ANTHEA PHARMA PRIVATE LIMITED]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=7a7c8579-d423-4699-9435-69c4d127c654"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"NSAID analgesic.","mechanism":"Nonselectively inhibits cyclooxygenase (COX-1/COX-2), reducing prostaglandin synthesis.","use":"Short-term treatment of moderately severe acute pain, often postoperative or trauma-related. Current ketorolac labeling limits total therapy duration to no more than 5 days in adults.","administration":"Use the exact ordered formulation and route. Current injectable ketorolac products may be IV or IM depending on strength/product; do not infer route from the generic name alone. Oral ketorolac, where used, is continuation therapy after IV/IM treatment rather than a separate long-course analgesic.","monitor":"• Assess pain before and after administration. • Watch for GI bleeding, abdominal pain, melena/hematemesis and other bleeding. • Review renal function, hydration and urine output. • Do not combine casually with another NSAID such as ibuprofen. • Review bleeding risk and perioperative context. • Escalate severe GI symptoms, bleeding, anaphylaxis/bronchospasm, marked fall in urine output, or suspected renal injury.","why":"Ketorolac inhibits COX enzymes and prostaglandin synthesis, producing strong non-opioid analgesia but also GI, platelet and renal risks.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 3 Oct 2026. Ketrolac is a ketorolac tromethamine brand; an injectable Ketrolac product is marketed as 30 mg/mL, but verify the exact ward product, strength and route before administration."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"NSAID analgesic.","id":"section-0-71667bd9507765ae"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Nonselectively inhibits cyclooxygenase (COX-1/COX-2), reducing prostaglandin synthesis.","id":"section-1-4b79a4654ee2a96b"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Short-term treatment of moderately severe acute pain, often postoperative or trauma-related. Current ketorolac labeling limits total therapy duration to no more than 5 days in adults.","id":"section-2-5cd96b4f56fe6e5b"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Use the exact ordered formulation and route. Current injectable ketorolac products may be IV or IM depending on strength/product; do not infer route from the generic name alone. Oral ketorolac, where used, is continuation therapy after IV/IM treatment rather than a separate long-course analgesic.","id":"section-3-b92280363cebd8ac"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Assess pain before and after administration. • Watch for GI bleeding, abdominal pain, melena/hematemesis and other bleeding. • Review renal function, hydration and urine output. • Do not combine casually with another NSAID such as ibuprofen. • Review bleeding risk and perioperative context. • Escalate severe GI symptoms, bleeding, anaphylaxis/bronchospasm, marked fall in urine output, or suspected renal injury.","id":"section-4-d1bc956a2a7a398d"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Ketorolac inhibits COX enzymes and prostaglandin synthesis, producing strong non-opioid analgesia but also GI, platelet and renal risks.","id":"section-5-033601651ee6446e"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 3 Oct 2026. Ketrolac is a ketorolac tromethamine brand; an injectable Ketrolac product is marketed as 30 mg/mL, but verify the exact ward product, strength and route before administration.","id":"section-6-dea53e87a2041950"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of COUMADIN — warfarin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"B","text":"Slows AV-nodal calcium-dependent conduction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"C","text":"Changes inflammatory/stress-response gene transcription","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis","explanation":"VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does COUMADIN — warfarin belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Unfractionated heparin","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"B","text":"Antiplatelet","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"C","text":"Fibrinolytic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"D","text":"Vitamin-K antagonist","explanation":"Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of COUMADIN — warfarin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Suppression of bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Relief of bronchospasm through airway receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Long-term anticoagulation for selected indications","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Long-term oral anticoagulation for selected thromboembolic indications. A key CCU example is a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including mechanical aortic valve replacement."},{"id":"D","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which result is used to adjust warfarin against the prescribed therapeutic target?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Serum potassium","explanation":"Potassium does not quantify warfarin effect."},{"id":"B","text":"INR","explanation":"INR is the routine warfarin anticoagulation measure."},{"id":"C","text":"Platelet count","explanation":"This measures cell quantity, not the warfarin anticoagulation intensity."},{"id":"D","text":"aPTT","explanation":"This is used for other coagulation assessments and some UFH protocols, not routine warfarin targeting."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Warfarin is monitored against an individually prescribed INR target.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_inr","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_inr"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which agent has a role in reversing warfarin’s effect under the appropriate clinical protocol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Vitamin K","explanation":"It supports restoration of vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis; the full reversal plan depends on severity."},{"id":"B","text":"Naloxone","explanation":"It reverses opioid effects, not vitamin-K antagonism."},{"id":"C","text":"Adenosine","explanation":"It transiently blocks AV-nodal conduction rather than reversing warfarin."},{"id":"D","text":"Atropine","explanation":"It treats selected bradycardia through muscarinic blockade."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Vitamin K has an indication-specific role in warfarin reversal.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_reversal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_reversal"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"During COUMADIN — warfarin therapy, a patient has new melena, pallor, dizziness, and a BP fall. Which concern best unifies the findings?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Clinically important blood loss","explanation":"The stool finding and hemodynamic change suggest bleeding with impaired perfusion."},{"id":"B","text":"A therapeutic reduction in coagulation alone","explanation":"Bleeding with circulatory symptoms is more than evidence of the intended drug action."},{"id":"C","text":"Uncomplicated postural intolerance","explanation":"This does not account for the new evidence of GI bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"Isolated vasodilator headache","explanation":"This does not explain melena and the systemic blood-loss pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize important bleeding during antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::bleeding"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A mechanical-valve patient is prescribed parenteral bridging while warfarin is established. Which pharmacodynamic distinction explains the overlap?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Warfarin directly activates plasmin to lyse existing thrombi","explanation":"That is a fibrinolytic mechanism, not the VKA mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"Warfarin reduces new factor synthesis while existing factors decline over time","explanation":"The full anticoagulant effect is delayed."},{"id":"C","text":"Warfarin irreversibly blocks platelet COX-1 before changing INR","explanation":"That describes aspirin’s primary platelet action."},{"id":"D","text":"Warfarin directly neutralizes circulating thrombin within seconds","explanation":"That is not warfarin’s action."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Warfarin’s full effect is delayed relative to administration.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--warfarin_delay","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_delay"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A warfarin-treated patient starts an interacting medicine or becomes acutely ill. Which follow-up most directly checks a changed anticoagulant response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pain score and joint movement","explanation":"These do not establish changed anticoagulation intensity."},{"id":"B","text":"SpO2 trend and inhaler technique","explanation":"These do not quantify the anticoagulant response."},{"id":"C","text":"QT interval and pulse-oximeter waveform","explanation":"These do not replace warfarin-specific monitoring."},{"id":"D","text":"INR trend and bleeding assessment","explanation":"Interaction or illness can alter warfarin response and bleeding risk."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Medicines, illness, and diet changes can alter warfarin response.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--warfarin_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_interactions"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Two mechanical-valve patients have different prescribed INR targets. Which difference can justify this?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Oral versus parenteral administration of warfarin maintenance","explanation":"The maintenance is oral and the target is not established this way."},{"id":"B","text":"Morning versus evening administration of the same warfarin dose","explanation":"Administration time does not define the valve-related target."},{"id":"C","text":"Valve type or position and patient thromboembolic risk","explanation":"These inform the patient-specific target."},{"id":"D","text":"Presence versus absence of a pre-dose meal","explanation":"Meal timing is not the basis of the valve-specific target."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Mechanical-valve anticoagulation targets depend on valve and patient factors.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--mechanical_valve","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::mechanical_valve"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Why does a mechanical valve create a continuing indication for prescribed anticoagulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Artificial non-endothelial surfaces and disturbed flow promote thrombus formation","explanation":"Anticoagulation reduces valve thrombosis and embolic risk."},{"id":"B","text":"The prosthesis produces a permanent vitamin-K deficiency","explanation":"The thrombogenic surface, not vitamin-K deficiency, explains the indication."},{"id":"C","text":"The prosthesis requires routine dissolution of its structural material","explanation":"Anticoagulation does not dissolve the valve itself."},{"id":"D","text":"The prosthesis prevents circulating platelets from reaching the heart","explanation":"This is not the mechanism of valve-related thrombosis."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Artificial valve surfaces and disturbed flow promote thrombosis and embolism.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--valve_thrombosis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::valve_thrombosis"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A warfarin patient's INR result returns. What is the appropriate comparison?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The platelet count reference interval","explanation":"INR and platelet count measure different aspects of hemostasis."},{"id":"B","text":"The individual's prescribed therapeutic INR target","explanation":"Targets depend on indication and patient factors."},{"id":"C","text":"A universal target for every patient","explanation":"One target does not fit every indication."},{"id":"D","text":"The normal range for an untreated person only","explanation":"Therapeutic anticoagulation is judged against its prescribed target."}],"point":"Warfarin is monitored against an individually prescribed INR target.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_inr","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_inr"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which new finding during COUMADIN — warfarin therapy needs prompt bleeding assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hematuria with dizziness and a falling BP","explanation":"Blood loss with circulatory symptoms can be significant."},{"id":"B","text":"A regular pulse with unchanged observations","explanation":"Stable observations alone are not a bleeding warning."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable hemoglobin with no reported symptoms","explanation":"This does not demonstrate a new bleeding event."},{"id":"D","text":"A healed venepuncture site without bruising","explanation":"This does not suggest current bleeding."}],"point":"Recognize important bleeding during antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::bleeding"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient asks why warfarin has not immediately produced its full effect. Which explanation fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Warfarin permanently removes circulating platelets","explanation":"Its principal mechanism is not platelet removal."},{"id":"B","text":"Warfarin works only while the tablet is in the stomach","explanation":"Its effect follows absorption and hepatic action."},{"id":"C","text":"Existing clotting factors must decline after synthesis is inhibited","explanation":"This explains the delayed anticoagulant effect."},{"id":"D","text":"Warfarin must first dissolve the existing thrombus","explanation":"It is not a direct clot-dissolving drug."}],"point":"Warfarin’s full effect is delayed relative to administration.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_delay","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_delay"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient stabilized on warfarin starts an interacting medicine. What follow-up is most relevant?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use a normal pulse to rule out interaction","explanation":"Pulse alone cannot establish anticoagulation safety."},{"id":"B","text":"Assume stable old doses guarantee a stable INR","explanation":"New medicines or illness can change the response."},{"id":"C","text":"Wait for the next annual review regardless of symptoms","explanation":"A new interaction can require earlier monitoring."},{"id":"D","text":"Review bleeding symptoms and the ordered INR plan","explanation":"Interactions can alter anticoagulant response."}],"point":"Medicines, illness, and diet changes can alter warfarin response.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_interactions"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":4}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ccu-warfarin-coumadin","title":"WARFARIN SODIUM TABLET [CARDINAL HEALTH 107, LLC]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=cdbbc317-35d3-42ba-94f0-c83891358529"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant.","mechanism":"Inhibits VKORC1, reducing regeneration of vitamin K and therefore synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S).","use":"Long-term oral anticoagulation for selected thromboembolic indications. A key CCU example is a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including mechanical aortic valve replacement.","administration":"PO exactly as ordered, with dosing adjusted according to INR and the prescribed therapeutic target. Do not treat a single universal INR target as correct for every mechanical valve; the target depends on valve type/position and patient thromboembolic risk. After mechanical-valve implantation, current ESC/EACTS guidance recommends starting UFH or LMWH bridging together with a VKA as soon as considered safe.","monitor":"• Check INR and the prescribed target range. • Assess for bleeding: gums, urine, stool, bruising, puncture sites, neurological symptoms or unexplained hypotension. • Review new medicines, antibiotics, diet changes and illness because they can change the INR. • Warfarin effect begins within about 24 hours, but peak anticoagulant effect is delayed about 72–96 hours; reaching a stable therapeutic INR can take longer. • Vitamin K is used to reverse warfarin effect in appropriate clinical situations according to severity/protocol.","why":"Warfarin inhibits vitamin-K recycling, reducing synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. Mechanical valves are thrombogenic because blood contacts an artificial non-endothelial surface and experiences disturbed flow, promoting thrombus formation; anticoagulation reduces valve thrombosis and systemic embolic risk.","availability":"WARD-REPORTED in the CCU at Nasser Medical Complex as Coumadin/warfarin. Exact tablet strength and current stock should be verified from the package/Kardex before administration."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant.","id":"section-0-d5ab4019e440ff56"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Inhibits VKORC1, reducing regeneration of vitamin K and therefore synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S).","id":"section-1-cb2ebc64244cec75"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Long-term oral anticoagulation for selected thromboembolic indications. A key CCU example is a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including mechanical aortic valve replacement.","id":"section-2-dffbfeb626e2fda3"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"PO exactly as ordered, with dosing adjusted according to INR and the prescribed therapeutic target. Do not treat a single universal INR target as correct for every mechanical valve; the target depends on valve type/position and patient thromboembolic risk. After mechanical-valve implantation, current ESC/EACTS guidance recommends starting UFH or LMWH bridging together with a VKA as soon as considered safe.","id":"section-3-8ea7fb5a1642f517"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Check INR and the prescribed target range. • Assess for bleeding: gums, urine, stool, bruising, puncture sites, neurological symptoms or unexplained hypotension. • Review new medicines, antibiotics, diet changes and illness because they can change the INR. • Warfarin effect begins within about 24 hours, but peak anticoagulant effect is delayed about 72–96 hours; reaching a stable therapeutic INR can take longer. • Vitamin K is used to reverse warfarin effect in appropriate clinical situations according to severity/protocol.","id":"section-4-5ac94bb30e8b3a3a"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Warfarin inhibits vitamin-K recycling, reducing synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. Mechanical valves are thrombogenic because blood contacts an artificial non-endothelial surface and experiences disturbed flow, promoting thrombus formation; anticoagulation reduces valve thrombosis and systemic embolic risk.","id":"section-5-cff7a468acc5301c"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-REPORTED in the CCU at Nasser Medical Complex as Coumadin/warfarin. Exact tablet strength and current stock should be verified from the package/Kardex before administration.","id":"section-6-dfaf3276c6fdeff1"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime","title":"FORTUM — ceftazidime","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard","itemId":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime","itemType":"medication","title":"FORTUM — ceftazidime","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of FORTUM — ceftazidime?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Airway anti-inflammatory and bronchodilator effects","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Ceftazidime is a third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"B","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"Ceftazidime is a third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduces angiotensin-II formation","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Ceftazidime is a third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"D","text":"Supplies rapidly available glucose","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Ceftazidime is a third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::principal-action"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does FORTUM — ceftazidime belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Carbapenem beta-lactam","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"B","text":"Polyene antifungal","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"C","text":"Third-generation cephalosporin","explanation":"Third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"D","text":"Nitroimidazole","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam antibacterial."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of FORTUM — ceftazidime?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Relief of bronchospasm by beta2 stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Treatment of susceptible serious Gram-negative infection","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Treatment of susceptible bacterial infections, including serious gram-negative infection and Pseudomonas when appropriate. In orthopaedic/trauma care it may be used for susceptible wound, soft-tissue or other serious infections according to cultures and the antimicrobial plan."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Minutes after starting FORTUM — ceftazidime, a patient develops wheeze, facial swelling, and hypotension. Which reaction best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Delayed marrow suppression","explanation":"This does not fit the rapid onset or the described manifestations."},{"id":"B","text":"Antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"This does not explain the immediate airway and circulatory pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Isolated infusion-site phlebitis","explanation":"A local vein reaction does not explain the multisystem findings."},{"id":"D","text":"Anaphylaxis","explanation":"Acute airway and circulatory compromise after exposure is strongly concerning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which result best informs assessment of accumulation risk and possible regimen adjustment for FORTUM — ceftazidime?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR trend and prothrombin time","explanation":"These assess a coagulation pathway rather than renal clearance."},{"id":"B","text":"ALT trend and serum bilirubin","explanation":"These assess hepatic injury rather than directly estimate renal clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"Creatinine trend and estimated GFR","explanation":"These inform renal clearance and regimen review."},{"id":"D","text":"TSH trend and free thyroxine","explanation":"These assess thyroid function rather than renal elimination."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"During FORTUM — ceftazidime therapy, which new bowel pattern most warrants assessment for antibiotic-associated colitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Frequent, persistent watery diarrhea","explanation":"Persistent diarrhea during antibiotic exposure needs assessment for C. difficile and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"Normal formed stool after breakfast","explanation":"This is not a new concerning diarrhea pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Hard stool with straining","explanation":"This points toward constipation rather than colitis-related diarrhea."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced stool frequency during immobility","explanation":"This does not identify the complication being tested."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which new neurological event during FORTUM — ceftazidime therapy needs the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An unchanged chronic headache","explanation":"The case does not describe a new neurological deterioration in this option."},{"id":"B","text":"A new generalized seizure","explanation":"This is a potentially serious neurological adverse effect or another emergency."},{"id":"C","text":"Mild unchanged nausea","explanation":"This is less urgent than a new seizure."},{"id":"D","text":"Improving original symptoms","explanation":"Therapeutic improvement does not itself identify neurotoxicity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A bacterial infection is being treated with FORTUM — ceftazidime. Which paired findings most directly inform whether the antimicrobial plan remains suitable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Infection response and culture susceptibility","explanation":"Clinical progress and organism susceptibility inform appropriateness."},{"id":"B","text":"Fluid balance and serum sodium","explanation":"These inform hydration but do not establish antimicrobial appropriateness."},{"id":"C","text":"Appetite and bowel frequency","explanation":"These inform general tolerance but not the organism-directed treatment decision."},{"id":"D","text":"Pain response and analgesic requirement","explanation":"These can improve without establishing antimicrobial susceptibility."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--standard--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":8},{"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime","itemType":"medication","title":"FORTUM — ceftazidime","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"Before FORTUM — ceftazidime, which prior antibiotic reaction carries the strongest immediate hypersensitivity concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Rapid wheeze and collapse after a beta-lactam","explanation":"The timing and cardiorespiratory involvement suggest a serious immediate reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Diarrhea several days into clindamycin therapy","explanation":"This needs review but is not the same immediate reaction pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Local IV discomfort without systemic symptoms","explanation":"This is less specific for a serious immediate systemic allergy."},{"id":"D","text":"Metallic taste during metronidazole therapy","explanation":"This is a different, commonly nonallergic adverse effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--advanced--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Renal clearance falls during FORTUM — ceftazidime therapy while the dose and interval remain unchanged. Which pharmacokinetic change is most concerning?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Decreased drug accumulation","explanation":"This is the opposite of the expected effect of reduced clearance."},{"id":"B","text":"A shorter elimination half-life","explanation":"Reduced clearance tends to prolong, not shorten, elimination."},{"id":"C","text":"A lower steady-state exposure","explanation":"With unchanged dosing, reduced clearance can increase exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Increased drug accumulation","explanation":"Reduced renal elimination can raise exposure between doses."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--advanced--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient develops persistent watery diarrhea after FORTUM — ceftazidime. Which explanation should be considered during assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An intended increase in osmotic laxative activity","explanation":"The antibiotic is not being used as an osmotic laxative."},{"id":"B","text":"Antibiotic-associated disturbance of intestinal flora","explanation":"This can permit C. difficile-associated disease."},{"id":"C","text":"An immediate IgE-mediated airway reaction","explanation":"The case describes a gastrointestinal pattern rather than acute anaphylaxis."},{"id":"D","text":"Therapeutic stimulation of intestinal glucose absorption","explanation":"This does not explain the antibiotic-associated diarrhea warning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--advanced--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A new seizure occurs during FORTUM — ceftazidime therapy. Which review most directly tests a drug-exposure explanation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Recent pain scores and the effectiveness of non-drug comfort measures","explanation":"These do not quantify the relevant drug exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"Recent wound appearance and the frequency of dressing changes","explanation":"These may inform infection but not the direct exposure hypothesis."},{"id":"C","text":"Recent dose, renal/hepatic clearance, and interacting medicines","explanation":"Exposure changes can increase neurotoxicity or alter seizure protection."},{"id":"D","text":"Recent stool pattern and the prescribed bowel regimen","explanation":"These do not address the principal drug-neurotoxicity explanation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--advanced--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime","itemType":"medication","title":"FORTUM — ceftazidime","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Shortly after FORTUM — ceftazidime, a patient develops wheeze, urticaria and hypotension. Which response takes priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Stop exposure and activate emergency anaphylaxis care","explanation":"Airway and circulatory compromise require an emergency response."},{"id":"B","text":"Treat the nausea before reassessing the wheeze","explanation":"Airway and circulatory threats take priority."},{"id":"C","text":"Slow administration and finish the prescribed dose","explanation":"Continued exposure can worsen a systemic reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Record a mild rash for review at discharge","explanation":"Wheeze and hypotension are not a mild isolated rash."}],"point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--7-day--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving FORTUM — ceftazidime develops acute kidney injury. Which prescription detail most needs review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose against the last normal creatinine only","explanation":"An old normal result does not establish current clearance."},{"id":"B","text":"Interval against serum potassium alone","explanation":"Potassium does not quantify the renal clearance needed for this review."},{"id":"C","text":"Dose against symptom relief alone","explanation":"Benefit does not exclude accumulation or a need for adjustment."},{"id":"D","text":"Dose and interval against current renal function","explanation":"Reduced clearance can require regimen adjustment."}],"point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--7-day--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After antibiotic treatment, a patient reports frequent watery stools and abdominal cramps. What is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Assume the infection is resolving through the bowel","explanation":"Diarrhea does not establish treatment success."},{"id":"B","text":"Assess and report possible antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"Significant diarrhea requires evaluation, including C. difficile when indicated."},{"id":"C","text":"Ignore it unless the next antibiotic dose is due","explanation":"Assessment is driven by symptoms, not the dosing time."},{"id":"D","text":"Recommend an antidiarrheal without further assessment","explanation":"Symptom suppression may be inappropriate before evaluating colitis."}],"point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--7-day--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A seizure occurs after a medicine change involving FORTUM — ceftazidime. Which review best addresses a medication contribution?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The original admission creatinine alone","explanation":"Renal function may have changed during treatment."},{"id":"B","text":"The previous seizure history without current exposure","explanation":"History matters but does not adequately assess a new medication contribution."},{"id":"C","text":"The most recent dose time without the regimen","explanation":"One timestamp does not establish exposure over time."},{"id":"D","text":"Recent doses, renal trend and interacting medicines","explanation":"These can reveal accumulation or an interaction."}],"point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--7-day--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After several doses of FORTUM — ceftazidime, fever persists and the patient deteriorates. What should guide the next review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The fever response to an antipyretic alone","explanation":"Temporary fever relief does not prove antimicrobial effectiveness."},{"id":"B","text":"The white cell count in isolation","explanation":"A single laboratory value does not replace the full clinical review."},{"id":"C","text":"Clinical course, cultures and the authorized treatment plan","explanation":"These help assess diagnosis, susceptibility and response."},{"id":"D","text":"An old culture from a different infection","explanation":"It may not represent the current pathogen or susceptibility."}],"point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime--7-day--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":5}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-fortum-ceftazidime","title":"CEFTAZIDIME INJECTION, POWDER, FOR SOLUTION [WG CRITICAL CARE, LLC]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=22d6534c-4488-48a1-8d78-e2fb908259de"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam antibacterial.","mechanism":"Beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","use":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infections, including serious gram-negative infection and Pseudomonas when appropriate. In orthopaedic/trauma care it may be used for susceptible wound, soft-tissue or other serious infections according to cultures and the antimicrobial plan.","administration":"Parenteral antibiotic. Reconstitution, dilution, route and infusion time depend on the exact vial/product and order. Check renal function because ceftazidime dosing requires adjustment in renal impairment.","monitor":"• Check cephalosporin/serious beta-lactam allergy history. • Monitor for rash, wheeze, hypotension or anaphylaxis. • Review renal function and prescribed dose. • Watch for significant diarrhea/C. difficile. • Neurologic toxicity such as confusion, myoclonus or seizures is more likely with accumulation, especially in renal impairment. • Review cultures and clinical response.","why":"Ceftazidime inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis. It has important gram-negative activity including Pseudomonas, but broad-spectrum use should remain culture/indication guided.","availability":"WARD-REPORTED in the current rotation. Palestinian MoH lists FORTUM injection 1 g as ceftazidime 1 g/vial. Fortum/ceftazidime has also been encountered in CCU practice; verify the exact vial strength and order in hand."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Third-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam antibacterial.","id":"section-0-9011b7c82aef4ccb"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","id":"section-1-0882368cf74cb4ca"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infections, including serious gram-negative infection and Pseudomonas when appropriate. In orthopaedic/trauma care it may be used for susceptible wound, soft-tissue or other serious infections according to cultures and the antimicrobial plan.","id":"section-2-62ba4cc6bb72cb82"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Parenteral antibiotic. Reconstitution, dilution, route and infusion time depend on the exact vial/product and order. Check renal function because ceftazidime dosing requires adjustment in renal impairment.","id":"section-3-b9b81a409152eee1"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Check cephalosporin/serious beta-lactam allergy history. • Monitor for rash, wheeze, hypotension or anaphylaxis. • Review renal function and prescribed dose. • Watch for significant diarrhea/C. difficile. • Neurologic toxicity such as confusion, myoclonus or seizures is more likely with accumulation, especially in renal impairment. • Review cultures and clinical response.","id":"section-4-4b622c18c55f2b6d"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Ceftazidime inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis. It has important gram-negative activity including Pseudomonas, but broad-spectrum use should remain culture/indication guided.","id":"section-5-dc1f6fec86c2e750"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-REPORTED in the current rotation. Palestinian MoH lists FORTUM injection 1 g as ceftazidime 1 g/vial. Fortum/ceftazidime has also been encountered in CCU practice; verify the exact vial strength and order in hand.","id":"section-6-4b7222c6e3a45d59"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide","title":"SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard","itemId":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide","itemType":"medication","title":"SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduces GI/GU smooth-muscle spasm","explanation":"Hyoscine butylbromide treats spasm without resolving every underlying cause of abdominal pain."},{"id":"B","text":"Inhibits serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Hyoscine butylbromide treats spasm without resolving every underlying cause of abdominal pain."},{"id":"C","text":"Predominantly alpha1-mediated vasoconstriction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Hyoscine butylbromide treats spasm without resolving every underlying cause of abdominal pain."},{"id":"D","text":"Receptor blockade and arterial dilation","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Hyoscine butylbromide treats spasm without resolving every underlying cause of abdominal pain."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Proton-pump inhibitor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Antimuscarinic antispasmodic."},{"id":"B","text":"Peripheral antimuscarinic","explanation":"Antimuscarinic antispasmodic."},{"id":"C","text":"D2 antagonist/prokinetic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Antimuscarinic antispasmodic."},{"id":"D","text":"First-generation H1 antagonist","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Antimuscarinic antispasmodic."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::drug-class"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Relief of GI or GU smooth-muscle spasm","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Relieves smooth-muscle spasm, such as gastrointestinal, biliary or urinary tract colic/spasm. It treats spasm-related pain; it does not treat the underlying cause of severe unexplained abdominal pain."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which pair of effects is consistent with the antimuscarinic component of SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Diarrhea and increased lacrimation","explanation":"These suggest increased rather than blocked muscarinic activity."},{"id":"B","text":"Salivation and urinary urgency","explanation":"These are opposite to the characteristic antimuscarinic effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Dry mouth and urinary retention","explanation":"Muscarinic blockade can reduce secretions and impair bladder emptying."},{"id":"D","text":"Miosis and bronchial secretions","explanation":"These do not fit the principal antimuscarinic pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize muscarinic-blockade adverse effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard--anticholinergic","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::anticholinergic"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which gastrointestinal history most directly requires clarification before SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Previously treated oral candidiasis","explanation":"This does not identify the relevant mechanical GI concern."},{"id":"B","text":"Previously controlled acid reflux","explanation":"This is a different gastrointestinal condition."},{"id":"C","text":"Mechanical bowel obstruction","explanation":"The drug’s GI action may be inappropriate or unsafe in this setting."},{"id":"D","text":"Previous uncomplicated dietary constipation","explanation":"This does not establish a mechanical obstruction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Stimulating GI motility may be unsafe in mechanical obstruction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard--gi_obstruction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::gi_obstruction"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which history is especially relevant before hyoscine butylbromide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Narrow-angle glaucoma with urinary-retention risk","explanation":"Both are relevant to antimuscarinic cautions."},{"id":"B","text":"Previously controlled fungal mouth infection","explanation":"This is unrelated to the principal antimuscarinic cautions."},{"id":"C","text":"Previously treated iron-deficiency anemia","explanation":"This does not identify the main antimuscarinic contraindication pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"An uncomplicated healed ankle fracture","explanation":"This does not establish the relevant drug risk."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review narrow-angle glaucoma, myasthenia, obstruction, and urinary retention risks.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard--antimuscarinic_cautions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::antimuscarinic_cautions"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide injection order is valid, but the minimum administration time is unclear. Which source should resolve preparation and rate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The instructions for a similar-looking vial on the trolley","explanation":"Appearance does not establish an equivalent product or rate."},{"id":"B","text":"The instructions for an oral product of the same name","explanation":"Oral instructions do not establish an IV administration rate."},{"id":"C","text":"The IV rate previously used for a different drug class","explanation":"Another drug’s administration rate is not transferable."},{"id":"D","text":"The exact product instructions and authorized local protocol","explanation":"Concentration, formulation, and indication can change administration requirements."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"IV administration speed follows the exact product and indication.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--standard--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::slow_iv"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":7},{"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide","itemType":"medication","title":"SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient prescribed SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide has worsening pain and distension with documented mechanical obstruction. Which reasoning best supports reviewing the order?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The presence of obstruction establishes a need for stronger prokinetic action","explanation":"Stimulation is not an appropriate assumption in a mechanically obstructed bowel."},{"id":"B","text":"The underlying obstruction changes the safety of symptomatic treatment","explanation":"The mechanism and cause matter beyond relief of symptoms."},{"id":"C","text":"The presence of pain establishes functional spasm as the cause","explanation":"Mechanical obstruction is already documented."},{"id":"D","text":"The presence of distension establishes inadequate acid suppression","explanation":"Distension does not establish an acid-related mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Stimulating GI motility may be unsafe in mechanical obstruction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--advanced--gi_obstruction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::gi_obstruction"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient receiving hyoscine butylbromide has persistent abdominal pain, fever, vomiting, and a BP fall. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A reduced analgesic effect establishes uncomplicated spasm","explanation":"The systemic red flags argue against that assumption."},{"id":"B","text":"A normal antimuscarinic response explains the fever","explanation":"Fever and deterioration are not established as a benign intended response."},{"id":"C","text":"A higher bowel-motility target explains the hypotension","explanation":"That does not account for the acute systemic presentation."},{"id":"D","text":"An underlying acute abdominal cause needs urgent assessment","explanation":"Antispasmodic use does not resolve or exclude serious pathology."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Do not use repeated antispasmodic relief to dismiss an acute abdominal cause.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--advanced--acute_abdomen","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::acute_abdomen"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":2},{"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide","itemType":"medication","title":"SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving SCOBUTYL — hyoscine-N-butyl bromide develops dry mouth, blurred vision and difficulty passing urine. Which pattern fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An isolated local skin reaction","explanation":"Several organ systems are involved, not just skin."},{"id":"B","text":"A selective fall in blood glucose","explanation":"The symptom pattern does not establish hypoglycemia."},{"id":"C","text":"A therapeutic increase in bladder emptying","explanation":"Urinary retention is an adverse effect, not improved emptying."},{"id":"D","text":"Antimuscarinic adverse effects","explanation":"Muscarinic blockade can affect salivation, vision and bladder emptying."}],"point":"Recognize muscarinic-blockade adverse effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--7-day--anticholinergic","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::anticholinergic"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Two injection products with the same drug name have different preparation instructions. Which should guide administration?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The remaining time in the medication round","explanation":"Convenience does not determine a safe administration rate."},{"id":"B","text":"The supplied product's instructions and authorized order","explanation":"Route, dilution and rate are product- and indication-specific."},{"id":"C","text":"The fastest rate used elsewhere on the ward","explanation":"Ward custom cannot replace verified instructions."},{"id":"D","text":"The rate remembered from another manufacturer's product","explanation":"Similar names do not guarantee identical preparation."}],"point":"IV administration speed follows the exact product and indication.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide--7-day--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-scobutyl-hyoscine-butylbromide::slow_iv"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":2}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"smPC-hyoscine","title":"Buscopan ampoules manufacturer SmPC","url":"https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/890/smpc"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Antimuscarinic antispasmodic.","mechanism":"Peripheral antimuscarinic action reduces smooth-muscle spasm in the GI and genitourinary tracts.","use":"Relieves smooth-muscle spasm, such as gastrointestinal, biliary or urinary tract colic/spasm. It treats spasm-related pain; it does not treat the underlying cause of severe unexplained abdominal pain.","administration":"Give only by the ordered formulation/route. Injectable hyoscine butylbromide products are commonly given slow IV or IM depending on the product and order; rapid IV administration can cause marked hypotension. Verify the local ampoule label before preparation.","monitor":"• Watch for tachycardia, dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention and blurred vision/accommodation problems. • Contraindications/major cautions include narrow-angle glaucoma, myasthenia gravis, mechanical GI obstruction/ileus and urinary retention risk. • Persistent or worsening abdominal pain with fever, vomiting, hypotension, syncope or blood in stool requires evaluation rather than repeated symptomatic dosing. • Parenteral doses can rarely cause anaphylaxis.","why":"Blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in peripheral smooth muscle, reducing gastrointestinal and genitourinary spasm.","availability":"WARD-REPORTED in the current rotation. Palestinian MoH lists SCOBUTYL ampoules containing hyoscine-N-butyl bromide 20 mg; verify ampoule volume, concentration and ordered route. Palestinian OTC records also list Scobutyl 10 mg tablets, so do not assume formulation from the brand name alone."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Antimuscarinic antispasmodic.","id":"section-0-d9273017864cdab8"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Peripheral antimuscarinic action reduces smooth-muscle spasm in the GI and genitourinary tracts.","id":"section-1-b2a2d33fdeda4760"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Relieves smooth-muscle spasm, such as gastrointestinal, biliary or urinary tract colic/spasm. It treats spasm-related pain; it does not treat the underlying cause of severe unexplained abdominal pain.","id":"section-2-e4b43e0015b7d875"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Give only by the ordered formulation/route. Injectable hyoscine butylbromide products are commonly given slow IV or IM depending on the product and order; rapid IV administration can cause marked hypotension. Verify the local ampoule label before preparation.","id":"section-3-9544473b7af2941a"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Watch for tachycardia, dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention and blurred vision/accommodation problems. • Contraindications/major cautions include narrow-angle glaucoma, myasthenia gravis, mechanical GI obstruction/ileus and urinary retention risk. • Persistent or worsening abdominal pain with fever, vomiting, hypotension, syncope or blood in stool requires evaluation rather than repeated symptomatic dosing. • Parenteral doses can rarely cause anaphylaxis.","id":"section-4-eeb99a4b0ad7df71"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in peripheral smooth muscle, reducing gastrointestinal and genitourinary spasm.","id":"section-5-f518060a773c84bf"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-REPORTED in the current rotation. Palestinian MoH lists SCOBUTYL ampoules containing hyoscine-N-butyl bromide 20 mg; verify ampoule volume, concentration and ordered route. Palestinian OTC records also list Scobutyl 10 mg tablets, so do not assume formulation from the brand name alone.","id":"section-6-387f86faf39b17da"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate","title":"VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--standard","itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate","itemType":"medication","title":"VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Enhances antithrombin inhibition of IIa/Xa","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."},{"id":"B","text":"Bronchodilation with a narrow therapeutic index","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."},{"id":"C","text":"Disrupts sterol-containing fungal membranes","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."},{"id":"D","text":"Nitric-oxide/cGMP-mediated vascular relaxation","explanation":"Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Vasopressor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Organic nitrate; long-acting antianginal."},{"id":"B","text":"Catecholamine","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Organic nitrate; long-acting antianginal."},{"id":"C","text":"Inotrope","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Organic nitrate; long-acting antianginal."},{"id":"D","text":"Organic nitrate","explanation":"Organic nitrate; long-acting antianginal."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prevention and control of angina","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Prevention/control of angina; not the rapid-rescue formulation for an acute angina attack."},{"id":"B","text":"Treatment of oral fungal lesions","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Suppression of gastric acid secretion","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient becomes light-headed on standing after VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate. Which paired assessment best checks the suspected adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Resting temperature and infection symptoms","explanation":"These investigate infection rather than the suspected pressure effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Postural BP and associated symptoms","explanation":"A positional pressure fall can account for the light-headedness."},{"id":"C","text":"Visual acuity and accommodation symptoms","explanation":"These evaluate visual effects rather than establish a postural pressure fall."},{"id":"D","text":"Bowel frequency and abdominal distension","explanation":"These investigate a gastrointestinal effect rather than postural circulatory tolerance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--standard--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::hypotension"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which symptom pattern is compatible with nitrate vasodilation during VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate therapy?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Nystagmus with slurred speech","explanation":"This suggests a different neurological toxicity pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Tendon pain with ankle swelling","explanation":"This points toward a different adverse-effect warning."},{"id":"C","text":"Headache with flushing or dizziness","explanation":"These are recognized nitrate effects; severity and BP still need assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Mucosal rash with blistering","explanation":"This raises a different serious reaction concern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Nitrate vasodilation commonly causes headache and dizziness.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--standard--nitrate_headache","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::nitrate_headache"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced","itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate","itemType":"medication","title":"VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient taking VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate has poor intake and a new BP fall when standing. Which factor best explains increased sensitivity to treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increased circulating volume","explanation":"This does not explain the volume-depleted presentation."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased vascular resistance","explanation":"This generally supports pressure rather than explaining the described fall."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced gastrointestinal transit","explanation":"Slower transit does not account for the positional hemodynamic change."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced circulating volume","explanation":"Volume depletion can amplify symptomatic hypotension."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::hypotension"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient due to receive VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate reports recent sildenafil use. What is the principal interaction concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Excessive vasodilation and hypotension","explanation":"The combination can produce a dangerous pressure fall."},{"id":"B","text":"Excessive gastric acid secretion","explanation":"This is not the clinically important interaction."},{"id":"C","text":"Excessive clotting-factor synthesis","explanation":"These drugs do not combine through vitamin-K-dependent coagulation."},{"id":"D","text":"Excessive bacterial protein-synthesis inhibition","explanation":"Neither drug is acting as an antibacterial."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"PDE5 inhibitors and nitrates can cause profound hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced--nitrate_pde5","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::nitrate_pde5"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with sudden chest pain asks to use a maintenance isosorbide mononitrate tablet as rescue. Which distinction matters?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An identical nitrate class establishing an identical onset","explanation":"Shared class does not make maintenance and rescue products equivalent."},{"id":"B","text":"The tablet’s total milligrams establishing the speed of relief","explanation":"Dose size alone does not establish an appropriate rescue onset."},{"id":"C","text":"Long-acting prevention versus the authorized acute chest-pain plan","explanation":"Maintenance oral mononitrate is not a rapid-rescue formulation."},{"id":"D","text":"The trade name alone rather than its formulation and intended role","explanation":"The formulation and indication are essential; a familiar name does not establish rapid rescue."}],"point":"Oral long-acting isosorbide mononitrate is preventive, not rapid rescue.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced--nitrate_rescue","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::nitrate_rescue"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"The prescribed VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."},{"id":"B","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."},{"id":"C","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."},{"id":"D","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::formulation"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day","itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate","itemType":"medication","title":"VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate, a patient feels faint during a transfer. What should the nurse do first?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Complete the transfer before assessing BP","explanation":"Continuing upright activity can worsen presyncope."},{"id":"B","text":"Help the patient sit or lie safely and assess BP","explanation":"Prevent a fall while checking circulatory tolerance."},{"id":"C","text":"Record dizziness and continue the medication round","explanation":"Documentation alone does not address current instability."},{"id":"D","text":"Offer a drink and reassess at the next round","explanation":"Symptoms require assessment now, not delayed review."}],"point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::hypotension"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient due for a nitrate reports recent PDE5-inhibitor use. What should the nurse do?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Clarify timing and obtain prescription review before giving it","explanation":"The combination can cause profound hypotension."},{"id":"B","text":"Give it with food to cancel the interaction","explanation":"Food does not cancel the hemodynamic interaction."},{"id":"C","text":"Rely on the usual dose to guarantee safety","explanation":"A usual dose can still interact dangerously."},{"id":"D","text":"Give it if the patient has no headache","explanation":"Absence of headache does not exclude the interaction."}],"point":"PDE5 inhibitors and nitrates can cause profound hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day--nitrate_pde5","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::nitrate_pde5"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient carries long-acting isosorbide mononitrate for angina. Which understanding is correct?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"It should be crushed whenever chest pain starts","explanation":"Crushing can be unsafe and does not establish a rescue plan."},{"id":"B","text":"It replaces urgent assessment for new persistent pain","explanation":"New concerning pain still requires assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"It acts like every sublingual nitrate product","explanation":"Formulations have different intended uses and kinetics."},{"id":"D","text":"It is maintenance treatment rather than rapid attack rescue","explanation":"Its role must not be confused with an acute rescue formulation."}],"point":"Oral long-acting isosorbide mononitrate is preventive, not rapid rescue.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day--nitrate_rescue","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::nitrate_rescue"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A supplied VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate product has a different route or release type from the prescription. What is the safest action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Substitute if the tablet strength looks similar","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish release or route suitability."},{"id":"B","text":"Crush the product to make absorption equivalent","explanation":"Crushing can alter release and may be unsafe."},{"id":"C","text":"Verify the exact product and authorized substitution","explanation":"Matching a drug name does not establish formulation equivalence."},{"id":"D","text":"Use the previous patient's preparation instructions","explanation":"Preparation must match this exact product and order."}],"point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::formulation"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":4}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate","title":"ISOSORBIDE MONONITRATE TABLET, EXTENDED RELEASE [AMERICAN HEALTH PACKAGING]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=6a582db5-eb45-4320-98c1-55517d7d8c38"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Organic nitrate; long-acting antianginal.","mechanism":"Releases nitric oxide → increases cGMP → vascular smooth-muscle relaxation.","use":"Prevention/control of angina; not the rapid-rescue formulation for an acute angina attack.","administration":"PO. No dilution, IV line or pump. Give the exact ordered formulation and schedule; do not assume immediate-release and modified-release products are interchangeable.","monitor":"• Check BP and dizziness before/after the dose. • Headache is common. • Never combine with sildenafil, tadalafil or vardenafil because profound hypotension/syncope can occur. • Verify whether the local tablet is immediate- or modified-release.","why":"Nitrate vasodilation lowers preload/BP; this oral long-acting form is mainly preventive.","availability":"YES for the generic/form. Palestinian essential-medicine data document isosorbide mononitrate 20-mg tablets. Vasocore is the ward-reported trade spelling and should be matched to the package."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Organic nitrate; long-acting antianginal.","id":"section-0-bc31fbae589da4de"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Releases nitric oxide → increases cGMP → vascular smooth-muscle relaxation.","id":"section-1-2355b974941891cf"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Prevention/control of angina; not the rapid-rescue formulation for an acute angina attack.","id":"section-2-ca0c22ea9aeb8b80"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"PO. No dilution, IV line or pump. Give the exact ordered formulation and schedule; do not assume immediate-release and modified-release products are interchangeable.","id":"section-3-dd1b771c40d2181e"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Check BP and dizziness before/after the dose. • Headache is common. • Never combine with sildenafil, tadalafil or vardenafil because profound hypotension/syncope can occur. • Verify whether the local tablet is immediate- or modified-release.","id":"section-4-ea600349289d45fb"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Nitrate vasodilation lowers preload/BP; this oral long-acting form is mainly preventive.","id":"section-5-38bbcf811933f97d"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"YES for the generic/form. Palestinian essential-medicine data document isosorbide mononitrate 20-mg tablets. Vasocore is the ward-reported trade spelling and should be matched to the package.","id":"section-6-a6e08055f4780434"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate","title":"CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--standard","itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate","itemType":"medication","title":"CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."},{"id":"B","text":"Antagonizes NMDA receptors","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."},{"id":"C","text":"Inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."},{"id":"D","text":"Nitric-oxide/cGMP-mediated vascular relaxation","explanation":"Nitrates relax vascular smooth muscle and reduce cardiac workload."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Catecholamine","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Organic nitrate vasodilator/antianginal."},{"id":"B","text":"Inotrope","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Organic nitrate vasodilator/antianginal."},{"id":"C","text":"Organic nitrate","explanation":"Organic nitrate vasodilator/antianginal."},{"id":"D","text":"Vasopressor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Organic nitrate vasodilator/antianginal."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Suppression of gastric acid secretion","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Treatment of oral fungal lesions","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Relief of angina using the prescribed nitrate formulation","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Faster nitrate relief than long-acting oral isosorbide mononitrate; IV nitrate therapy may be used for severe/unstable angina or refractory LV failure when specifically ordered."},{"id":"D","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A patient becomes light-headed on standing after CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate. Which paired assessment best checks the suspected adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Visual acuity and accommodation symptoms","explanation":"These evaluate visual effects rather than establish a postural pressure fall."},{"id":"B","text":"Postural BP and associated symptoms","explanation":"A positional pressure fall can account for the light-headedness."},{"id":"C","text":"Resting temperature and infection symptoms","explanation":"These investigate infection rather than the suspected pressure effect."},{"id":"D","text":"Bowel frequency and abdominal distension","explanation":"These investigate a gastrointestinal effect rather than postural circulatory tolerance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--standard--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::hypotension"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which symptom pattern is compatible with nitrate vasodilation during CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate therapy?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Nystagmus with slurred speech","explanation":"This suggests a different neurological toxicity pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Headache with flushing or dizziness","explanation":"These are recognized nitrate effects; severity and BP still need assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Tendon pain with ankle swelling","explanation":"This points toward a different adverse-effect warning."},{"id":"D","text":"Mucosal rash with blistering","explanation":"This raises a different serious reaction concern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Nitrate vasodilation commonly causes headache and dizziness.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--standard--nitrate_headache","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::nitrate_headache"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"The documented CORDIL product is a sublingual tablet. Which administration matches that formulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Let it dissolve under the tongue","explanation":"This uses the supplied sublingual formulation as intended."},{"id":"B","text":"Crush it into a nebulizer chamber","explanation":"This is not an inhaled formulation."},{"id":"C","text":"Dissolve it for a controlled IV infusion","explanation":"A sublingual tablet is not an IV product."},{"id":"D","text":"Swallow it as a modified-release maintenance tablet","explanation":"That assumes a different formulation and absorption route."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use the specified sublingual nitrate formulation by its intended route.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--standard--sl_tablet","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::sl_tablet"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--advanced","itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate","itemType":"medication","title":"CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient taking CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate has poor intake and a new BP fall when standing. Which factor best explains increased sensitivity to treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced gastrointestinal transit","explanation":"Slower transit does not account for the positional hemodynamic change."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced circulating volume","explanation":"Volume depletion can amplify symptomatic hypotension."},{"id":"C","text":"Increased circulating volume","explanation":"This does not explain the volume-depleted presentation."},{"id":"D","text":"Increased vascular resistance","explanation":"This generally supports pressure rather than explaining the described fall."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--advanced--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::hypotension"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient due to receive CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate reports recent sildenafil use. What is the principal interaction concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Excessive bacterial protein-synthesis inhibition","explanation":"Neither drug is acting as an antibacterial."},{"id":"B","text":"Excessive clotting-factor synthesis","explanation":"These drugs do not combine through vitamin-K-dependent coagulation."},{"id":"C","text":"Excessive gastric acid secretion","explanation":"This is not the clinically important interaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Excessive vasodilation and hypotension","explanation":"The combination can produce a dangerous pressure fall."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"PDE5 inhibitors and nitrates can cause profound hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--advanced--nitrate_pde5","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::nitrate_pde5"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"The prescribed CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."},{"id":"B","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."},{"id":"C","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."},{"id":"D","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--advanced--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::formulation"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":3},{"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--7-day","itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate","itemType":"medication","title":"CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate, a patient feels faint during a transfer. What should the nurse do first?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Record dizziness and continue the medication round","explanation":"Documentation alone does not address current instability."},{"id":"B","text":"Help the patient sit or lie safely and assess BP","explanation":"Prevent a fall while checking circulatory tolerance."},{"id":"C","text":"Offer a drink and reassess at the next round","explanation":"Symptoms require assessment now, not delayed review."},{"id":"D","text":"Complete the transfer before assessing BP","explanation":"Continuing upright activity can worsen presyncope."}],"point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--7-day--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::hypotension"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient due for a nitrate reports recent PDE5-inhibitor use. What should the nurse do?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Give it with food to cancel the interaction","explanation":"Food does not cancel the hemodynamic interaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Rely on the usual dose to guarantee safety","explanation":"A usual dose can still interact dangerously."},{"id":"C","text":"Give it if the patient has no headache","explanation":"Absence of headache does not exclude the interaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Clarify timing and obtain prescription review before giving it","explanation":"The combination can cause profound hypotension."}],"point":"PDE5 inhibitors and nitrates can cause profound hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--7-day--nitrate_pde5","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::nitrate_pde5"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A supplied CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate product has a different route or release type from the prescription. What is the safest action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Substitute if the tablet strength looks similar","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish release or route suitability."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the previous patient's preparation instructions","explanation":"Preparation must match this exact product and order."},{"id":"C","text":"Verify the exact product and authorized substitution","explanation":"Matching a drug name does not establish formulation equivalence."},{"id":"D","text":"Crush the product to make absorption equivalent","explanation":"Crushing can alter release and may be unsafe."}],"point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--7-day--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::formulation"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":3,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate","title":"ISOSORBIDE DINITRATE TABLET [PAR HEALTH USA, LLC]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=6df6a2fa-c56c-4eb6-a485-c16585420eb0"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Organic nitrate vasodilator/antianginal.","mechanism":"Nitric-oxide/cGMP-mediated vasodilation.","use":"Faster nitrate relief than long-acting oral isosorbide mononitrate; IV nitrate therapy may be used for severe/unstable angina or refractory LV failure when specifically ordered.","administration":"For the documented SL product, place under the tongue and let dissolve. Do not treat an SL tablet as an IV product. IV ISDN exists internationally as a controlled infusion, so any IV vial must be verified from the exact product before use.","monitor":"• Check BP before and after use. • Headache, flushing and dizziness are common. • Check for PDE-5 inhibitor exposure. • If an IV nitrate is ordered, verify the exact product, concentration, tubing and infusion method.","why":"Route determines onset and delivery; nitrate vasodilation can rapidly lower BP.","availabi